[{:db/id 17592186124509 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d68af87f-be02-55a7-9b78-0da04db4e309"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-d68af87f-be02-55a7-9b78-0da04db4e309" :question/id #uuid "d68af87f-be02-55a7-9b78-0da04db4e309" :question/math-academy-id "q-79345" :question/problem "Consider the matrices given below. Find $\\text{dim(Null}(A))+\\text{dim(Null}(B))+\\text{dim(Null}(C)).$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The dimension of the null space of a matrix is the number of non-pivot columns in the matrix. \n\nAlso, all of the given matrices are in row echelon form.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the statements.\n\n- The matrix $A$ has $1$ pivot column and $2$ non-pivot columns (namely, the $2$nd and $3$rd columns). So, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(A))=2.\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ has $3$ pivot columns and $1$ non-pivot column (namely, the $4$th column). So, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(B))=1.\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ has $3$ pivot columns and $1$ non-pivot column (namely, the $3$rd column). So, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(C))=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(A))+\\text{dim(Null}(B))+\\text{dim(Null}(C))=2+1+1=4.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f77f73c8-a6f1-5686-8eae-81626a3ac1d9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f77f73c8-a6f1-5686-8eae-81626a3ac1d9" :answer-field/id #uuid "f77f73c8-a6f1-5686-8eae-81626a3ac1d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-787219f1-94ee-53fb-a49b-8777fcdb7957" :answer-field/choices ["answer-75415968-8129-50a3-b6ce-101666b52758" "answer-982cdb38-b85b-58e9-9088-665bbeac8223" "answer-223b7f24-1bf6-5866-97dd-305c2bfc6faa" "answer-787219f1-94ee-53fb-a49b-8777fcdb7957" "answer-feb82dbb-03db-5058-b53a-495b57a7a067"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-75415968-8129-50a3-b6ce-101666b52758" :answer/id #uuid "75415968-8129-50a3-b6ce-101666b52758" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-982cdb38-b85b-58e9-9088-665bbeac8223" :answer/id #uuid "982cdb38-b85b-58e9-9088-665bbeac8223" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-223b7f24-1bf6-5866-97dd-305c2bfc6faa" :answer/id #uuid "223b7f24-1bf6-5866-97dd-305c2bfc6faa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-787219f1-94ee-53fb-a49b-8777fcdb7957" :answer/id #uuid "787219f1-94ee-53fb-a49b-8777fcdb7957" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-feb82dbb-03db-5058-b53a-495b57a7a067" :answer/id #uuid "feb82dbb-03db-5058-b53a-495b57a7a067" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-9ba31b31-2601-5ef2-b2a4-48c8b616b8cf" :activity/id #uuid "9ba31b31-2601-5ef2-b2a4-48c8b616b8cf" :activity/title "The Dimension of the Null Space of a Matrix" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050996 :activity/course 17592186046658 :activity/steps ["step-00e75ee6-51f2-5e0e-83f6-e87b9422ad7c" "step-0aa973af-27e4-5410-b0a4-b621f52c8916" "step-b5d981f1-253c-5ca8-ac7c-2553c528663e" "step-8ef6e9a0-a726-58b5-b34b-a12fe19773a7" "step-c00cfbd0-d98b-5d36-a27e-24a7e17590cf"] :activity/first-step "step-00e75ee6-51f2-5e0e-83f6-e87b9422ad7c" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-00e75ee6-51f2-5e0e-83f6-e87b9422ad7c" :step/id #uuid "00e75ee6-51f2-5e0e-83f6-e87b9422ad7c" :step/content 17592186124867 :step/next "step-0aa973af-27e4-5410-b0a4-b621f52c8916" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-0aa973af-27e4-5410-b0a4-b621f52c8916" :step/id #uuid "0aa973af-27e4-5410-b0a4-b621f52c8916" :step/content 17592186124868 :step/next "step-b5d981f1-253c-5ca8-ac7c-2553c528663e" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-b5d981f1-253c-5ca8-ac7c-2553c528663e" :step/id #uuid "b5d981f1-253c-5ca8-ac7c-2553c528663e" :step/content 17592186319318 :step/next "step-8ef6e9a0-a726-58b5-b34b-a12fe19773a7" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-8ef6e9a0-a726-58b5-b34b-a12fe19773a7" :step/id #uuid "8ef6e9a0-a726-58b5-b34b-a12fe19773a7" :step/content 17592186425404 :step/next "step-c00cfbd0-d98b-5d36-a27e-24a7e17590cf" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-c00cfbd0-d98b-5d36-a27e-24a7e17590cf" :step/id #uuid "c00cfbd0-d98b-5d36-a27e-24a7e17590cf" :step/content "question-d68af87f-be02-55a7-9b78-0da04db4e309" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186100029 :answer-field/choices 17592186100032] [:db/retract 17592186100029 :answer-field/choices 17592186100033] [:db/retract 17592186100029 :answer-field/choices 17592186100034] [:db/retract 17592186100029 :answer-field/choices 17592186100030] [:db/retract 17592186100029 :answer-field/choices 17592186100031] {:db/id 17592186100845 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e33bb576-2a78-5fb1-8965-49bc676e2392" "question-a9e9c021-fc29-5d89-a4ff-166a4f01dd84"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186101478 :question/problem "![](images/eb/ebe962cc3394e0839d77fba922a5a7f56f31201f6926ef8b4236c1f82edd2e90.png)\n\nConsider a right triangle inscribed in a semicircle of radius\n$5,$ as shown above. What is the greatest possible perimeter of the triangle?" :question/worked-solution "Let's call $l$ the length of one of the legs. By the Pythagorean theorem, the length of the other leg is $\\sqrt{100-{l}^{2}}.$\n\nTherefore, the perimeter is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(l) & =10+l+\\sqrt{100-{l}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $l$ is the length of a leg of a right triangle whose hypotenuse is $10,$ the permitted range of $l$ is $0<l<10.$\n\nWe want to maximize $P(l),$ so we need to compute the critical points. Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(l) & =1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{-2l}{\\sqrt{100-{l}^{2}}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{l}{\\sqrt{100-{l}^{2}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(l)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(l) & =0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{l}{\\sqrt{100-{l}^{2}}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{l}{\\sqrt{100-{l}^{2}}} & =1 \\\\ l & =\\sqrt{100-{l}^{2}} \\\\ {l}^{2} & =100-{l}^{2} \\\\ 2{l}^{2} & =100 \\\\ {l}^{2} & =50 \\\\ l & =5\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll that's left now is to confirm that $l=5\\sqrt{2}$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(l).$ To do this, we use the first derivative test. We write up a table of values:\n\n| Interval | $(0,5\\sqrt{2})$ | $5\\sqrt{2}$ | $(5\\sqrt{2},10)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $1$ | $$ | $8$ |\n| ${P}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $P$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n\nSo $l=5\\sqrt{2}$ does indeed give a maximum, and the perimeter of the resulting triangle will be the largest possible.\n\nTherefore, the greatest perimeter that a triangle can have is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(5\\sqrt{2}) & =10+5\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{100-(5\\sqrt{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =10+5\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{100-50} \\\\  & =10+5\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{50} \\\\  & =10+5\\sqrt{2}+5\\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =10+10\\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =10(1+\\sqrt{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100029 :answer-field/correct "answer-dade38b2-d19e-5e1f-a69c-803c56722835" :answer-field/choices ["answer-bbdb68bd-b484-51c4-a434-8a6450e1553a" "answer-602e5323-47b5-567c-ad05-2e0f14c6bd42" "answer-dade38b2-d19e-5e1f-a69c-803c56722835" "answer-81a1d39e-ffa3-5851-9280-ef0749290fad" "answer-16d0883d-37ec-5524-9e37-0ebd4d8a596b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bbdb68bd-b484-51c4-a434-8a6450e1553a" :answer/id #uuid "bbdb68bd-b484-51c4-a434-8a6450e1553a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10(1+2\\sqrt{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-602e5323-47b5-567c-ad05-2e0f14c6bd42" :answer/id #uuid "602e5323-47b5-567c-ad05-2e0f14c6bd42" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10(1+3\\sqrt{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dade38b2-d19e-5e1f-a69c-803c56722835" :answer/id #uuid "dade38b2-d19e-5e1f-a69c-803c56722835" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10(1+\\sqrt{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-81a1d39e-ffa3-5851-9280-ef0749290fad" :answer/id #uuid "81a1d39e-ffa3-5851-9280-ef0749290fad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10(1+\\sqrt{3})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-16d0883d-37ec-5524-9e37-0ebd4d8a596b" :answer/id #uuid "16d0883d-37ec-5524-9e37-0ebd4d8a596b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5(1+4\\sqrt{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e33bb576-2a78-5fb1-8965-49bc676e2392" :question/id #uuid "e33bb576-2a78-5fb1-8965-49bc676e2392" :question/math-academy-id "q-84644" :question/problem "![](images/64/643d8218715b65def0d9fd274c50b18c76cfbea35cd3dc70f3cdbb5c5b16b6f8.png)\n\nConsider a right triangle inscribed in a semicircle of radius\n$2,$ as shown above. What is the greatest possible perimeter of the triangle?" :question/worked-solution "Let's call $l$ the length of one of the legs. By the Pythagorean theorem, the length of the other leg is $\\sqrt{16-{l}^{2}}.$\n\nTherefore, the perimeter is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(l) & =4+l+\\sqrt{16-{l}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $l$ is the length of a leg of a right triangle whose hypotenuse is $4,$ the permitted range of $l$ is $0<l<4.$\n\nWe want to maximize $P(l),$ so we need to compute the critical points. Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(l) & =1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{-2l}{\\sqrt{16-{l}^{2}}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{l}{\\sqrt{16-{l}^{2}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(l)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(l) & =0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{l}{\\sqrt{16-{l}^{2}}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{l}{\\sqrt{16-{l}^{2}}} & =1 \\\\ l & =\\sqrt{16-{l}^{2}} \\\\ {l}^{2} & =16-{l}^{2} \\\\ 2{l}^{2} & =16 \\\\ {l}^{2} & =8 \\\\ l & =2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll that's left now is to confirm that $l=2\\sqrt{2}$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(l).$ To do this, we use the first derivative test. We write up a table of values:\n\n| Interval | $(0,2\\sqrt{2})$ | $2\\sqrt{2}$ | $(2\\sqrt{2},4)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $1$ | $$ | $3$ |\n| ${P}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $P$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n\nSo, $l=2\\sqrt{2}$ does indeed give a maximum, and the perimeter of the resulting triangle will be the largest possible.\n\nTherefore, the greatest perimeter that a triangle can have is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2\\sqrt{2}) & =4+2\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{16-(2\\sqrt{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =4+2\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{16-8} \\\\  & =4+2\\sqrt{2}+2\\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =4+4\\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =4(1+\\sqrt{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7d676937-e9eb-5b1f-9a5a-f6981fa3e2b6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7d676937-e9eb-5b1f-9a5a-f6981fa3e2b6" :answer-field/id #uuid "7d676937-e9eb-5b1f-9a5a-f6981fa3e2b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-165e90cc-3b0d-5a02-a5a3-8e2d3d8588ed" :answer-field/choices ["answer-35dfa7d4-3b02-58c0-ad0c-6812b20c5dd1" "answer-165e90cc-3b0d-5a02-a5a3-8e2d3d8588ed" "answer-b08dd26b-63ed-5c34-a8e4-74444069711e" "answer-c1eabd82-920e-5aa0-b2c1-c63c5538278b" "answer-ef21318a-633e-5e82-8e9e-01f49a6a7ff5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-35dfa7d4-3b02-58c0-ad0c-6812b20c5dd1" :answer/id #uuid "35dfa7d4-3b02-58c0-ad0c-6812b20c5dd1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4(1+2\\sqrt{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-165e90cc-3b0d-5a02-a5a3-8e2d3d8588ed" :answer/id #uuid "165e90cc-3b0d-5a02-a5a3-8e2d3d8588ed" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4(1+\\sqrt{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b08dd26b-63ed-5c34-a8e4-74444069711e" :answer/id #uuid "b08dd26b-63ed-5c34-a8e4-74444069711e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4(2+\\sqrt{3})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c1eabd82-920e-5aa0-b2c1-c63c5538278b" :answer/id #uuid "c1eabd82-920e-5aa0-b2c1-c63c5538278b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8(1+\\sqrt{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ef21318a-633e-5e82-8e9e-01f49a6a7ff5" :answer/id #uuid "ef21318a-633e-5e82-8e9e-01f49a6a7ff5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8(1+\\sqrt{3})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a9e9c021-fc29-5d89-a4ff-166a4f01dd84" :question/id #uuid "a9e9c021-fc29-5d89-a4ff-166a4f01dd84" :question/math-academy-id "q-84649" :question/problem "Consider a right triangle inscribed in a semicircle of radius\n$5\\sqrt{2}.$ If the hypotenuse of the triangle lies along the diameter of the semicircle, what is the greatest possible perimeter of the triangle?" :question/worked-solution "We first draw a picture of the situation:\n\n![](images/29/296e866a3104f0d2a7891726274578bcdffb84853ce896e8723e2b742d22ac27.png)\n\nLet's call $l$ the length of one of the legs. By the Pythagorean theorem, the length of the other leg is $\\sqrt{200-{l}^{2}}.$\n\nTherefore, the perimeter is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(l) & =10\\sqrt{2}+l+\\sqrt{200-{l}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $l$ is the length of a leg of a right triangle whose hypotenuse is $10\\sqrt{2},$ the permitted range of $l$ is $0<l<10\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nWe want to maximize $P(l),$ so we need to compute the critical points. Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(l) & =1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{-2l}{\\sqrt{200-{l}^{2}}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{l}{\\sqrt{200-{l}^{2}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(l)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(l) & =0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{l}{\\sqrt{200-{l}^{2}}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{l}{\\sqrt{200-{l}^{2}}} & =1 \\\\ l & =\\sqrt{200-{l}^{2}} \\\\ {l}^{2} & =200-{l}^{2} \\\\ 2{l}^{2} & =200 \\\\ {l}^{2} & =100 \\\\ l & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll that's left now is to confirm that $l=10$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(l).$ To do this, we use the first derivative test. We write up a table of values:\n\n| Interval | $(0,10)$ | $10$ | $(10,10\\sqrt{2})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $1$ | $$ | $11$ |\n| ${P}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $P$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n\nSo, $l=10$ does indeed give a maximum, and the perimeter of the resulting triangle will be the largest possible.\n\nTherefore, the greatest perimeter that a triangle can have is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(10) & =10\\sqrt{2}+10+\\sqrt{200-{10}^{2}} \\\\  & =10\\sqrt{2}+10+\\sqrt{200-100} \\\\  & =10\\sqrt{2}+10+\\sqrt{100} \\\\  & =10\\sqrt{2}+10+10 \\\\  & =10\\sqrt{2}+20 \\\\  & =10(2+\\sqrt{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-280fb089-0f58-509d-8499-e6519dccfdda"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-280fb089-0f58-509d-8499-e6519dccfdda" :answer-field/id #uuid "280fb089-0f58-509d-8499-e6519dccfdda" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f92bd0b5-e08f-58bd-9107-acbcd75c75d1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2877d8e6-e960-54e3-9caf-592270a7f040" "answer-f92bd0b5-e08f-58bd-9107-acbcd75c75d1" "answer-b7211e4d-0ac5-51f4-aaeb-258dc346a3b6" "answer-26b31079-d767-507c-b7c2-8e2d5e35817a" "answer-65db8df0-61db-5c03-a942-ca2140d41e26"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2877d8e6-e960-54e3-9caf-592270a7f040" :answer/id #uuid "2877d8e6-e960-54e3-9caf-592270a7f040" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10(1+\\sqrt{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f92bd0b5-e08f-58bd-9107-acbcd75c75d1" :answer/id #uuid "f92bd0b5-e08f-58bd-9107-acbcd75c75d1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10(2+\\sqrt{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b7211e4d-0ac5-51f4-aaeb-258dc346a3b6" :answer/id #uuid "b7211e4d-0ac5-51f4-aaeb-258dc346a3b6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "20(1+2\\sqrt{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-26b31079-d767-507c-b7c2-8e2d5e35817a" :answer/id #uuid "26b31079-d767-507c-b7c2-8e2d5e35817a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "20(1+\\sqrt{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-65db8df0-61db-5c03-a942-ca2140d41e26" :answer/id #uuid "65db8df0-61db-5c03-a942-ca2140d41e26" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5(4+\\sqrt{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100944 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-63d7b221-5dbf-5fb1-b55e-24803c54cfbf" "question-7ba89d20-58fc-5947-9d86-d842de2f0086"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-63d7b221-5dbf-5fb1-b55e-24803c54cfbf" :question/id #uuid "63d7b221-5dbf-5fb1-b55e-24803c54cfbf" :question/math-academy-id "q-86380" :question/problem "Consider a right triangle inscribed in a semicircle of radius $10\\sqrt{2}.$ If the hypotenuse of the triangle lies along the diameter of the semicircle, find the maximum possible area of the triangle." :question/worked-solution "We first draw a picture of the situation:\n\n![](images/17/172826a7fa27ac8957f120e15622cf0c96b0e8df66e5f6fd3d7d99ab0b32c5f0.png)\n\nLet's call $l$ the length of one leg. By the Pythagorean theorem, the length of the other leg is $\\sqrt{800-{l}^{2}}.$\n\nTherefore, the area of the triangle is given by\n\n$$\nA(l)=\\frac{1}{2}l\\sqrt{800-{l}^{2}}\\,.\n$$\n\nSince $l$ is the length of a leg of a right triangle whose hypotenuse is $20\\sqrt{2}$, the permitted range of $l$ is $0<l<20\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nWe want to maximize $A(l),$ so we need to compute the critical points. Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(l) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{800-{l}^{2}}-\\frac{{l}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{800-{l}^{2}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${A}^{′}(l)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(l) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{800-{l}^{2}}-\\frac{{l}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{800-{l}^{2}}} & =0 \\\\ 800-{l}^{2}-{l}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {l}^{2} & =\\frac{800}{2} \\\\ {l}^{2} & =400 \\\\ l & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll that's left now is to confirm that $l=20$ does indeed give a maximum value of $A(l).$ To do this, we use the first derivative test. We write up a table of values:\n\n| Interval | $(0,20)$ | $20$ | $(20,20\\sqrt{2})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $1$ | $$ | $25$ |\n| ${A}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $A$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n\nSo, $l=20$ does indeed give a maximum, and the area of the resulting triangle will be the largest possible.\n\nFinally, the maximum possible area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(20) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 20\\cdot \\sqrt{800-{(20)}^{2}} \\\\  & =10\\cdot \\sqrt{800-400} \\\\  & =10\\cdot \\sqrt{400} \\\\  & =10\\cdot 20 \\\\  & =200.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-04564310-aef1-5572-80f0-0e88d49a5152"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-04564310-aef1-5572-80f0-0e88d49a5152" :answer-field/id #uuid "04564310-aef1-5572-80f0-0e88d49a5152" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-904dde19-8dc3-5432-80ad-2c1c137aafba" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5319f4ad-15cf-555e-aa17-f39d7212a5e8" "answer-e2149821-7718-5f16-85d2-5599e78ce659" "answer-904dde19-8dc3-5432-80ad-2c1c137aafba" "answer-d92ad90d-da23-5fff-85e4-1da26bf2a05e" "answer-50ba2075-ff36-56d5-92fd-c143a5d90b53"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5319f4ad-15cf-555e-aa17-f39d7212a5e8" :answer/id #uuid "5319f4ad-15cf-555e-aa17-f39d7212a5e8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "120\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e2149821-7718-5f16-85d2-5599e78ce659" :answer/id #uuid "e2149821-7718-5f16-85d2-5599e78ce659" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "160" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-904dde19-8dc3-5432-80ad-2c1c137aafba" :answer/id #uuid "904dde19-8dc3-5432-80ad-2c1c137aafba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "200" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d92ad90d-da23-5fff-85e4-1da26bf2a05e" :answer/id #uuid "d92ad90d-da23-5fff-85e4-1da26bf2a05e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "280" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-50ba2075-ff36-56d5-92fd-c143a5d90b53" :answer/id #uuid "50ba2075-ff36-56d5-92fd-c143a5d90b53" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "80\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-7ba89d20-58fc-5947-9d86-d842de2f0086" :question/id #uuid "7ba89d20-58fc-5947-9d86-d842de2f0086" :question/math-academy-id "q-344587" :question/problem "![](images/f8/f88b6c506d909cce64eef23c71acaed15e382c55571f02048e0c9c616d75544e.png)\n\nConsider a right triangle inscribed in a semicircle of radius $8,$ as shown above. Find the maximum possible area ${A}_{\\text{max}}$  of the triangle. Do not round the answer.\n\n${A}_{\\text{max}}=$ \n {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's call $l$ the length of one leg. By the Pythagorean theorem, the length of the other leg is $\\sqrt{256-{l}^{2}}.$\n\nTherefore, the area of the triangle is given by\n\n$$\nA(l)=\\frac{1}{2}l\\sqrt{256-{l}^{2}}\\,.\n$$\n\nSince $l$ is the length of a leg of a right triangle whose hypotenuse is $16$, the permitted range of $l$ is $0<l<16.$\n\nWe want to maximize $A(l),$ so we need to compute the critical points. Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{A}^{′}(l)=\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{256-{l}^{2}}-\\frac{{l}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{256-{l}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nand solving ${A}^{′}(l)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{256-{l}^{2}}-\\frac{{l}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{256-{l}^{2}}} & =0 \\\\ 256-{l}^{2}-{l}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 2{l}^{2} & =256 \\\\ {l}^{2} & =128 \\\\ l & =8\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll that's left now is to confirm that $l=8\\sqrt{2}$ does indeed give a maximum value of $A(l).$ To do this, we use the first derivative test. We write up a table of values:\n\n| Interval | $(0,8\\sqrt{2})$ | $8\\sqrt{2}$ | $(8\\sqrt{2},16)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $1$ | $$ | $12$ |\n| ${A}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $A$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n\nSo, $l=8\\sqrt{2}$ does indeed give a maximum, and the area of the resulting triangle will be the largest possible.\n\nFinally, the maximum possible area ${A}_{\\text{max}}$  is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{\\text{max}}=A(8\\sqrt{2}) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 8\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{256-(8\\sqrt{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 8\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{128} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 8\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 8\\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 64\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =64.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c876964c-ac3b-5bc2-8e7a-39e0baa2db2f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c876964c-ac3b-5bc2-8e7a-39e0baa2db2f" :answer-field/id #uuid "c876964c-ac3b-5bc2-8e7a-39e0baa2db2f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-ef243c68-032d-58b6-99d9-14742212f3e5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ef243c68-032d-58b6-99d9-14742212f3e5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ef243c68-032d-58b6-99d9-14742212f3e5" :answer/id #uuid "ef243c68-032d-58b6-99d9-14742212f3e5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "64" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-5ff9603d-5460-59ff-9729-a46115941f92" :activity/id #uuid "5ff9603d-5460-59ff-9729-a46115941f92" :activity/title "Optimization Problems With Inscribed Shapes" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050853 :activity/course 17592186046645 :activity/steps ["step-4b1dd13e-8324-5c94-8b7a-ea2f2a32528c" "step-4860ba8b-5ab3-5a16-a3b5-ae2ce3460d7a" "step-f4095f70-f0b7-5cc2-862e-308f0d5ba0f6" "step-f32d82f2-d987-5e57-b419-1fae08ca9e6b" "step-dc87c367-1f65-5bbf-b227-588c8c6ebf57"] :activity/first-step "step-4b1dd13e-8324-5c94-8b7a-ea2f2a32528c" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-4b1dd13e-8324-5c94-8b7a-ea2f2a32528c" :step/id #uuid "4b1dd13e-8324-5c94-8b7a-ea2f2a32528c" :step/content 17592186101478 :step/next "step-4860ba8b-5ab3-5a16-a3b5-ae2ce3460d7a" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-4860ba8b-5ab3-5a16-a3b5-ae2ce3460d7a" :step/id #uuid "4860ba8b-5ab3-5a16-a3b5-ae2ce3460d7a" :step/content "question-e33bb576-2a78-5fb1-8965-49bc676e2392" :step/next "step-f4095f70-f0b7-5cc2-862e-308f0d5ba0f6" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-f4095f70-f0b7-5cc2-862e-308f0d5ba0f6" :step/id #uuid "f4095f70-f0b7-5cc2-862e-308f0d5ba0f6" :step/content "question-a9e9c021-fc29-5d89-a4ff-166a4f01dd84" :step/next "step-f32d82f2-d987-5e57-b419-1fae08ca9e6b" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-f32d82f2-d987-5e57-b419-1fae08ca9e6b" :step/id #uuid "f32d82f2-d987-5e57-b419-1fae08ca9e6b" :step/content "question-63d7b221-5dbf-5fb1-b55e-24803c54cfbf" :step/next "step-dc87c367-1f65-5bbf-b227-588c8c6ebf57" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-dc87c367-1f65-5bbf-b227-588c8c6ebf57" :step/id #uuid "dc87c367-1f65-5bbf-b227-588c8c6ebf57" :step/content "question-7ba89d20-58fc-5947-9d86-d842de2f0086" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186134291 :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}2x\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the type III region given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,{x}^{2}\\le z\\le 2{x}^{2},\\,0\\le y\\le 2xz\\}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133925 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-78d73ab6-dc58-5b09-8f0f-74be4ef20e0b"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186134127 :question/worked-solution "![](images/c1/c1853c772e219f53a0b394bd0b50beea6b33c451afc453983cdccb1df333c5ea.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that our solid can be written as the type III region\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 4-x-z\\},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\nis the projection of $R$ onto the $xz$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/06/0620f8f25174cb71528049341669458c68a7ccfdd3ff76b5f62c878307080419.png)\n\nHence, the type III representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 4-x-z\\}\n$$\n\nAs a result, by writing down our triple integral as a repeated integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}12xz\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4-x-z}12xz\\,dy\\,dz\\,dx.\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $y$, treating $x$ and $z$ as constants: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4-x-z}12xz\\,dy\\,dz\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{4-x-z}12xz\\,dy]dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}12xz[y]_{y=0}^{y=4-x-z}\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}12xz(4-x-z)\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(48xz-12{x}^{2}z-12x{z}^{2})\\,dz\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $z$, treating $x$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(48xz-12{x}^{2}z-12x{z}^{2})\\,dz\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}(48xz-12{x}^{2}z-12x{z}^{2})\\,dz]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[24x{z}^{2}-6{x}^{2}{z}^{2}-4x{z}^{3}]_{z=0}^{z=2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(64x-24{x}^{2})\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(64x-24{x}^{2})\\,dx & ={[32{x}^{2}-8{x}^{3}]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =(32-8)-0 \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186281291 :question/problem "Evaluate the mixed integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}6y\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}A\n$$\n\ngiven that the region $D$ in the $xz$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 1-x,\\,0\\le x\\le 1\\}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186281319 :question/problem "Evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}4x\\,dV\n$$\n\nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 1\\},\n$$\n\nand $D$ is a finite region in $xz$-plane enclosed between the $x$-axis, the $z$-axis, and the curve $z+{x}^{2}=1$ in the first quadrant." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-78d73ab6-dc58-5b09-8f0f-74be4ef20e0b" :question/id #uuid "78d73ab6-dc58-5b09-8f0f-74be4ef20e0b" :question/math-academy-id "q-164349" :question/problem "![](images/78/78c5b7d059cac1d2ad94edf93a3d9317a62e5c1d1c88a4be29c49bbe9dac5f39.png)\n\nGiven that $R$ is the type III region shown above, \n${∭}_{R}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V=$" :question/worked-solution "For a type III region $R$ with projection $D$ onto the $xz$-plane, the triple integral of a continuous function $f(x,y,z)$ over $R$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,z)}^{{u}_{2}(x,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe consider the region $R$ as a type III region enclosed between the back surface $y={u}_{1}(x,z)=2$ and the front surface $y={u}_{2}(x,z)=6.$\n\n![](images/a0/a03d6986874a543bc21d274433b5268444f2487aea198834d379f004a7d85e79.png)\n\nSo, the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,2\\le y\\le 6\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ as a type I *plane* region in the $xz$-plane with the upper curve\n\n$$\nz=1-x.\n$$\n\n![](images/06/06e8986d493efbe696751db410f84490ba634c9f68feaa03aa0bf9d71593feb3.png)\n\nHence, $D=\\{(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le z\\le 1-x\\}.$\n\nTherefore, we write the triple integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{2}^{6}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}[{\\int }_{2}^{6}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}{x}^{2}[y]_{y=2}^{y=6}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}4{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b4d91747-a788-57b5-b27b-550a878336f2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b4d91747-a788-57b5-b27b-550a878336f2" :answer-field/id #uuid "b4d91747-a788-57b5-b27b-550a878336f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e774e34d-ed51-5d51-b55f-fe1ccb615316" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e774e34d-ed51-5d51-b55f-fe1ccb615316" "answer-3a1dfd9a-580f-544f-8d77-20ab90892309" "answer-8f4d7c9b-5084-5084-8274-90591e28fab4" "answer-423f4b08-f254-5350-bbe9-5a945e23b082" "answer-b3177ad2-c14f-5b29-b1fb-67e4d067ea11"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e774e34d-ed51-5d51-b55f-fe1ccb615316" :answer/id #uuid "e774e34d-ed51-5d51-b55f-fe1ccb615316" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}4{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3a1dfd9a-580f-544f-8d77-20ab90892309" :answer/id #uuid "3a1dfd9a-580f-544f-8d77-20ab90892309" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8f4d7c9b-5084-5084-8274-90591e28fab4" :answer/id #uuid "8f4d7c9b-5084-5084-8274-90591e28fab4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}4{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-423f4b08-f254-5350-bbe9-5a945e23b082" :answer/id #uuid "423f4b08-f254-5350-bbe9-5a945e23b082" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b3177ad2-c14f-5b29-b1fb-67e4d067ea11" :answer/id #uuid "b3177ad2-c14f-5b29-b1fb-67e4d067ea11" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}8xz\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-b2253863-a2eb-5d06-8e91-3826f6e8edbf" :activity/id #uuid "b2253863-a2eb-5d06-8e91-3826f6e8edbf" :activity/title "Triple Integrals Over Type III Regions" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050165 :activity/course 17592186046660 :activity/steps ["step-584a6f1d-7daa-56a7-b438-a33d62c99067" "step-d4f52355-bc41-52e2-acb1-84fccf2c578e" "step-7daf2a73-2425-568d-93d8-9ecb67de0d98" "step-c04ddbb0-06ea-5d6c-a8b3-e6a0600ff993" "step-95b50fbb-45c2-5a7d-a86b-c6b9c74dc2fa"] :activity/first-step "step-584a6f1d-7daa-56a7-b438-a33d62c99067" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-584a6f1d-7daa-56a7-b438-a33d62c99067" :step/id #uuid "584a6f1d-7daa-56a7-b438-a33d62c99067" :step/content 17592186134291 :step/next "step-d4f52355-bc41-52e2-acb1-84fccf2c578e" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-d4f52355-bc41-52e2-acb1-84fccf2c578e" :step/id #uuid "d4f52355-bc41-52e2-acb1-84fccf2c578e" :step/content 17592186134127 :step/next "step-7daf2a73-2425-568d-93d8-9ecb67de0d98" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-7daf2a73-2425-568d-93d8-9ecb67de0d98" :step/id #uuid "7daf2a73-2425-568d-93d8-9ecb67de0d98" :step/content 17592186281291 :step/next "step-c04ddbb0-06ea-5d6c-a8b3-e6a0600ff993" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-c04ddbb0-06ea-5d6c-a8b3-e6a0600ff993" :step/id #uuid "c04ddbb0-06ea-5d6c-a8b3-e6a0600ff993" :step/content 17592186281319 :step/next "step-95b50fbb-45c2-5a7d-a86b-c6b9c74dc2fa" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-95b50fbb-45c2-5a7d-a86b-c6b9c74dc2fa" :step/id #uuid "95b50fbb-45c2-5a7d-a86b-c6b9c74dc2fa" :step/content "question-78d73ab6-dc58-5b09-8f0f-74be4ef20e0b" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124525 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-7b9e6797-8ad6-50af-aa3b-75e9f9aa6e0e"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-7b9e6797-8ad6-50af-aa3b-75e9f9aa6e0e" :question/id #uuid "7b9e6797-8ad6-50af-aa3b-75e9f9aa6e0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-96689" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 11 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 8 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above. Which of the following is a basis of the eigenspace corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =7?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =7.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 11 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 8 & 5\\end{aligned}]-7[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 8 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}0=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+8{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 4 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 8 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 8 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 4 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 8 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are free variables. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-4{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}.$ So, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-4{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required basis is\n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3112cc9b-c99d-5760-8709-44ca37572265"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3112cc9b-c99d-5760-8709-44ca37572265" :answer-field/id #uuid "3112cc9b-c99d-5760-8709-44ca37572265" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f7b099a0-b60b-53de-9964-15d50b6188cc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f7b099a0-b60b-53de-9964-15d50b6188cc" "answer-754b876d-1c1d-5fa2-8c8a-19930e540b4b" "answer-e516614c-5556-5ad9-a091-72667d82e048" "answer-1d1456d1-c217-5333-894e-c3d90b3be143" "answer-f4ce0150-7f6b-597d-9302-bb72b972307e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f7b099a0-b60b-53de-9964-15d50b6188cc" :answer/id #uuid "f7b099a0-b60b-53de-9964-15d50b6188cc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-754b876d-1c1d-5fa2-8c8a-19930e540b4b" :answer/id #uuid "754b876d-1c1d-5fa2-8c8a-19930e540b4b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e516614c-5556-5ad9-a091-72667d82e048" :answer/id #uuid "e516614c-5556-5ad9-a091-72667d82e048" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1d1456d1-c217-5333-894e-c3d90b3be143" :answer/id #uuid "1d1456d1-c217-5333-894e-c3d90b3be143" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f4ce0150-7f6b-597d-9302-bb72b972307e" :answer/id #uuid "f4ce0150-7f6b-597d-9302-bb72b972307e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-db699c01-90c1-5ada-b014-7431e2333415" :activity/id #uuid "db699c01-90c1-5ada-b014-7431e2333415" :activity/title "Calculating the Eigenvectors of a 3x3 Matrix in the General Case" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050857 :activity/course 17592186046658 :activity/steps ["step-a646c6d2-ebc3-59d4-84e2-5a1b718c10ae" "step-fa858142-a712-54c2-946c-6b7b0fe5ba70" "step-d601707f-3fdc-5047-9b04-055887e649a4" "step-2ad2b66f-c218-5745-9878-11331d3e9cab" "step-95da3e94-779f-5413-a325-1e56abe1dbec"] :activity/first-step "step-a646c6d2-ebc3-59d4-84e2-5a1b718c10ae" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-a646c6d2-ebc3-59d4-84e2-5a1b718c10ae" :step/id #uuid "a646c6d2-ebc3-59d4-84e2-5a1b718c10ae" :step/content 17592186125009 :step/next "step-fa858142-a712-54c2-946c-6b7b0fe5ba70" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-fa858142-a712-54c2-946c-6b7b0fe5ba70" :step/id #uuid "fa858142-a712-54c2-946c-6b7b0fe5ba70" :step/content 17592186360694 :step/next "step-d601707f-3fdc-5047-9b04-055887e649a4" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-d601707f-3fdc-5047-9b04-055887e649a4" :step/id #uuid "d601707f-3fdc-5047-9b04-055887e649a4" :step/content "question-7b9e6797-8ad6-50af-aa3b-75e9f9aa6e0e" :step/next "step-2ad2b66f-c218-5745-9878-11331d3e9cab" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-2ad2b66f-c218-5745-9878-11331d3e9cab" :step/id #uuid "2ad2b66f-c218-5745-9878-11331d3e9cab" :step/content 17592186360697 :step/next "step-95da3e94-779f-5413-a325-1e56abe1dbec" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-95da3e94-779f-5413-a325-1e56abe1dbec" :step/id #uuid "95da3e94-779f-5413-a325-1e56abe1dbec" :step/content 17592186432015 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186150041 :answer-field/choices 17592186150046] {:db/id 17592186152178 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-2f37d338-5ba5-5919-bf66-41519f983a1f"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186151973 :question/worked-solution "Consider the point $U.$ We can take an open interval of $x$-values such that $U$ will be the highest point on the graph in this interval. So, $U$ is a local maximum.\n\nA similar interval exists for the point $W,$ too. So, $W$ is also a local maximum.\n\n![](images/ac/ac0a6e28e4564883e6743b2d600cb8611723e813e85c1acb5fcc327ee3dbc9b5.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152216 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-2dea474c-b14e-5037-9ce9-358952e01aab" "question-95d717c9-1435-5503-b9b6-aa0cf5a741dc" "question-ea412ed1-a3cb-5bcc-876d-3ca4d60883c9"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152026 :question/problem "For which of the following values of $x$ does the function $y = f(x),$ shown below, attain a local minimum?\n\nI. $x=0$\n\nII. $x=1$\n\nIII. $x=3$\n\n![](images/17/1718b48d39255b221037a439cc48d300a04c33ffb4e04c700fefb303a20ebff2.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given points in turn.\n\n- The function has a local minimum at $x=0.$ For example, in the interval $(-0.5,0.5),$ the smallest value of the function is attained at $x=0,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/e2/e25e6e31d6ea5d7f882d4eed16c82ec8280d1ed56f275974e8f07091c0e62c5d.png)\n\nWe can write this mathematically as $f(x) \\ge f(0) \\:$ for all $\\: x \\in (-0.5,0.5).$\n\n- The function does *not* have a local minimum at $x=1.$ For any open interval containing $x=1$, there will be points on the graph that lie below $f(1)$.\n\n- The function has a local minimum at $x=3.$ For example, in the interval $(2.5,3.5),$ the smallest value of the function is attained at $x=3,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/d1/d133d7ee4c022b47740f62b8e73ba9ea2f70e788861c25d2824b5e7236027c60.png)\n\nWe can write this mathematically as $f(x) \\ge f(3) \\:$ for all $\\: x \\in (2.5,3.5).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152217 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a017d206-18fc-5343-8dce-b59fb9d7e098" "question-50fb8efe-0b0b-50a4-80a5-fe5eb2cee4f9"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152027 :question/problem "Let $f(x)$ be the function shown below. Which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. $f(x)$ attains a local maximum at $x=-2$\n\nII. $f(x)$ attains a local minimum at $x=2$\n\nIII. $f(x)$ attains a local maximum at $x=0$\n\n![](images/9b/9b933b1b869b74f2a7340f9ba4034b970c49434d12592d3ff1e152057b6adef1.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The function has a local minimum at $x=-2.$ For example, in the interval $(-2.5,-1.5),$ the smallest value of the function is attained at $x=-2,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/3a/3abb7470db51fa1bdb61e15aec1739de44bf5083b7341b90a65ac93fc7f8adbb.png)\n\nWe can write this mathematically as $f(x) \\ge f(-2) \\:$ for all $\\: x \\in (-2.5,-1.5).$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function has a local minimum at $x=2.$ For example, in the interval $(1.5,2.5),$ the smallest value of the function is attained at $x=2,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/71/71c6e7af0a1de77543b57d2aee242fe84295ad46c748b2f65f7cba381c47b80c.png)\n\nWe can write this mathematically as $f(x) \\ge f(2) \\:$ for all $\\: x \\in (1.5,2.5).$\n\n- Statement III is true. The function has a local maximum at $x=0.$ For example, in the interval $(-0.5,0.5),$ the greatest value of the function is attained at $x=0,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/83/83de300c3e00766d46989d12b4e1708ef9a73f4d02748009a526b1646d7f0b81.png)\n\nWe can write this mathematically as $f(x) \\le f(0) \\:$ for all $\\: x \\in (-0.5,0.5).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152218 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e60840d8-8ef2-5027-842a-3130fbad5883" "question-f8983cc1-bd12-59ce-89e8-0458cbfae2b2" "question-f3f7c8b0-18ee-5d7c-bcd5-63f6de1aef9a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152028 :question/problem "The graph of a function $y = f(x)$ is shown below. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $f(x)$ attains a local minimum at $x=2$\n\nII. $f(x)$ attains a **global** maximum at $x=0$\n\nIII. $f(x)$ attains a local minimum at $x=-2$\n\n![](images/91/91d9d4997dd58c2c3cb07efc1529487bbd9a5a2f5c611054289cfc2ba24d764c.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function has a local minimum at $x=2.$ For example, in the interval $(1.5,2.5),$ the smallest value of the function is attained at $x=2,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/00/00a21a670b2080ce766ddf142eb2f37303d7af7b667e1b5038eee93879c071f7.png)\n\n \nWe can write this mathematically as $f(x) \\ge f(2) \\:$ for all $\\: x \\in (1.5,2.5).$\n\n- Statement II is false. The function does *not* have a global maximum at $x=0.$ The global maximum is the highest point on the graph, but our function has points where its value is greater than $f(0).$ For example, $f(0) \\lt f(3).$\n\n- Statement III is true. The function has a local minimum at $x=-2.$ For example, in the interval $(-2.5,-1.5),$ the smallest value of the function is attained at $x=-2,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/fc/fc96fe5bb938b0ca8569b864588348a4d22280e3c4a5806cdd20af6eb4bcea34.png)\n\n \nWe can write this mathematically as $f(x) \\ge f(-2) \\:$ for all $\\: x \\in (-2.5,-1.5).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152589 :question/problem "![](images/20/20b6e19fcb7e1fe4f33697d517c28d9563c793634cfbcd5552d842efeef3ef61.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the points shown are the local maxima of the function?" :question/worked-solution "Consider the point $B.$ We can take an open interval of $x$-values such that $B$ will be the highest point on the graph in this interval. Hence, $B$ is a local maximum.\n\nA similar interval exists for the point $D,$ too. Hence, $D$ is also a local maximum.\n\n![](images/26/260f30379f71c006b43ec6eb26200ced27a4b40aec938140d6a4cd06910ba02c.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186150041 :answer-field/correct 17592186150042 :answer-field/choices ["answer-d8aa7e0f-0e11-569d-b62c-d63a9933f854"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d8aa7e0f-0e11-569d-b62c-d63a9933f854" :answer/id #uuid "d8aa7e0f-0e11-569d-b62c-d63a9933f854" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$A,$ $C,$ and $E$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-2f37d338-5ba5-5919-bf66-41519f983a1f" :question/id #uuid "2f37d338-5ba5-5919-bf66-41519f983a1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-37221" :question/problem "![](images/bb/bbe19cf01b4ca415802db1d505b402e2c238aad6c443170cf18cd7b79d7f238a.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the points shown are the local maxima of the function?" :question/worked-solution "Consider the point $Q.$ We can take an open interval of $x$-values such that $Q$ will be the highest point on the graph in this interval. Hence, $Q$ is a local maximum.\n\n![](images/14/14d2e4c17f5a79165c2e6b1988fe949625eccb87d2880b1131705028be5884c4.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5cd7ae0b-7ec2-5c51-90c1-c5404e7bfe82"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5cd7ae0b-7ec2-5c51-90c1-c5404e7bfe82" :answer-field/id #uuid "5cd7ae0b-7ec2-5c51-90c1-c5404e7bfe82" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a35da3ca-b24a-5b99-9f39-e50f455e8703" :answer-field/choices ["answer-36521294-d221-5ad2-8696-e1f86f442121" "answer-69fe4d81-06a7-5434-b1d4-340a3b5c51f3" "answer-a8b9c53e-daef-5f47-bdc8-019568ba167a" "answer-a35da3ca-b24a-5b99-9f39-e50f455e8703" "answer-abeec097-a753-588f-8ea5-dd4de32b9330"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-36521294-d221-5ad2-8696-e1f86f442121" :answer/id #uuid "36521294-d221-5ad2-8696-e1f86f442121" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$P$ and $R$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-69fe4d81-06a7-5434-b1d4-340a3b5c51f3" :answer/id #uuid "69fe4d81-06a7-5434-b1d4-340a3b5c51f3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$P$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a8b9c53e-daef-5f47-bdc8-019568ba167a" :answer/id #uuid "a8b9c53e-daef-5f47-bdc8-019568ba167a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$Q$ and $R$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a35da3ca-b24a-5b99-9f39-e50f455e8703" :answer/id #uuid "a35da3ca-b24a-5b99-9f39-e50f455e8703" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$Q$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-abeec097-a753-588f-8ea5-dd4de32b9330" :answer/id #uuid "abeec097-a753-588f-8ea5-dd4de32b9330" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The function does not have local maxima" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-2dea474c-b14e-5037-9ce9-358952e01aab" :question/id #uuid "2dea474c-b14e-5037-9ce9-358952e01aab" :question/math-academy-id "q-37056" :question/problem "![](images/d6/d65013cac950b3e3d533dd6dfbdf1dc6582c10fcb7d80f00ab559935915d9595.png)\n\nFor which of the following values of $x$ does the function $y=f(x),$ shown above, attain a local maximum?\n\nI. $x=-1$\n\nII. $x=1$\n\nIII. $x=3$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given points in turn.\n\n- The function has a local maximum at $x=-1.$ For example, in the interval $(-1.25,-0.75),$ the greatest value of the function is attained at $x=-1,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/a5/a5f0e23d8bed98f693ee72086b0bf17b9f65de8931bba6f16cfcc34e670a757e.png)\n\nWe can write this mathematically as $f(x)\\le f(-1)\\,$ for all $\\,x\\in (-1.25,-0.75).$\n\n- The function does *not* have a local maximum at $x=1.$ For any open interval containing $x=1,$ there will be points on the graph that lie above $f(1).$\n\n- The function does *not* have a local maximum at $x=3.$ For any open interval containing $x=3,$ there will be points on the graph that lie above $f(3).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-90e3bd18-2e16-599f-bb1b-9383fb750717"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-90e3bd18-2e16-599f-bb1b-9383fb750717" :answer-field/id #uuid "90e3bd18-2e16-599f-bb1b-9383fb750717" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ecc307b1-edce-5714-bc4c-64086844ce1c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e94ee3c8-4200-57dc-b3b1-c38a4cadf223" "answer-959452b5-53ec-5e51-b5b3-ddee4c8025f8" "answer-ecc307b1-edce-5714-bc4c-64086844ce1c" "answer-78dfb633-9488-5239-95f7-c6301808bf02" "answer-1b806a24-f26f-55a2-80e1-991117d5b9ad"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e94ee3c8-4200-57dc-b3b1-c38a4cadf223" :answer/id #uuid "e94ee3c8-4200-57dc-b3b1-c38a4cadf223" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-959452b5-53ec-5e51-b5b3-ddee4c8025f8" :answer/id #uuid "959452b5-53ec-5e51-b5b3-ddee4c8025f8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ecc307b1-edce-5714-bc4c-64086844ce1c" :answer/id #uuid "ecc307b1-edce-5714-bc4c-64086844ce1c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-78dfb633-9488-5239-95f7-c6301808bf02" :answer/id #uuid "78dfb633-9488-5239-95f7-c6301808bf02" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1b806a24-f26f-55a2-80e1-991117d5b9ad" :answer/id #uuid "1b806a24-f26f-55a2-80e1-991117d5b9ad" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-95d717c9-1435-5503-b9b6-aa0cf5a741dc" :question/id #uuid "95d717c9-1435-5503-b9b6-aa0cf5a741dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-108276" :question/problem "![](images/d7/d71ebdb084e23aef4b3901aaba2e4bb4de5438fa41a3fc331bef749f3c78de76.png)\n\nFor which of the following values of $x$ does the function $y=f(x),$ shown above, attain a local minimum?\n\nI. $x=-4$\n\nII. $x=-1$\n\nIII. $x=3$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given points in turn.\n\n- The function does *not* have a local minimum at $x=-4.$ For any open interval containing $x=-4$, there will be points on the graph that lie below $f(-4).$\n\n- The function does *not* have a local minimum at $x=-1.$ For any open interval containing $x=-1$, there will be points on the graph that lie below $f(-1).$\n\n- The function has a local minimum at $x=3.$ For example, in the interval $(2.5,3.5),$ the smallest value of the function is attained at $x=3,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/bf/bfe274fe02b7200164eaad5c98aa2b19297738987f70059157d2895b989a962c.png)\n\nWe can write this mathematically as $f(x)\\ge f(3)\\,$ for all $\\,x\\in (2.5,3.5).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-73e50eb5-bf95-5c3b-ba5b-752a51e4d2de"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-73e50eb5-bf95-5c3b-ba5b-752a51e4d2de" :answer-field/id #uuid "73e50eb5-bf95-5c3b-ba5b-752a51e4d2de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8221aaeb-03bd-5c7f-bfbe-01b407418a5a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0ff7a2f1-6a9d-58b0-9489-95fdb0edebdd" "answer-274536c7-1482-5982-a5d8-6f7cb683a480" "answer-6dffbcb2-fa18-58fe-981c-593b53989224" "answer-4fdedf1e-85b6-5c7a-b28c-1c13f89e5434" "answer-8221aaeb-03bd-5c7f-bfbe-01b407418a5a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0ff7a2f1-6a9d-58b0-9489-95fdb0edebdd" :answer/id #uuid "0ff7a2f1-6a9d-58b0-9489-95fdb0edebdd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-274536c7-1482-5982-a5d8-6f7cb683a480" :answer/id #uuid "274536c7-1482-5982-a5d8-6f7cb683a480" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6dffbcb2-fa18-58fe-981c-593b53989224" :answer/id #uuid "6dffbcb2-fa18-58fe-981c-593b53989224" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4fdedf1e-85b6-5c7a-b28c-1c13f89e5434" :answer/id #uuid "4fdedf1e-85b6-5c7a-b28c-1c13f89e5434" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8221aaeb-03bd-5c7f-bfbe-01b407418a5a" :answer/id #uuid "8221aaeb-03bd-5c7f-bfbe-01b407418a5a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152594 :question/problem "![](images/c4/c46e88b9e044fccb91e0efa4ee981973a286e0335f58a70f7b1f9a856d9bfed4.png)\n\nFor which of the following values of $x$ does the function $y=f(x),$ shown above, attain a local minimum?\n\nI. $x=3$\n\nII. $x=2$\n\nIII. $x=-2$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given points in turn.\n\n- The function has a local minimum at $x=3.$ For example, in the interval $(2.75,3.25),$ the smallest value of the function is attained at $x=3,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/61/6115a87572fb93b9e3dcf69c75cd4a5efaae2dbbea90b28cb57cdd05b8db11b7.png)\n\nWe can write this mathematically as $f(x)\\ge f(3)\\,$ for all $\\,x\\in (2.75,3.25).$\n\n- The function does *not* have a local minimum at $x=2.$ For any open interval containing $x=2$, there will be points on the graph that lie below $f(2).$\n\n- The function has a local minimum at $x=-2.$ For example, in the interval $(-2.5,-1.5),$ the smallest value of the function is attained at $x=-2,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/26/26acee415fa4a1038077d11f88b7c61c8b45f4aa857cc578af192e4085f7c005.png)\n\nWe can write this mathematically as $f(x)\\ge f(2)\\,$ for all $\\,x\\in (-2.5,-1.5).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186150071 :answer-field/correct 17592186150075 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-ea412ed1-a3cb-5bcc-876d-3ca4d60883c9" :question/id #uuid "ea412ed1-a3cb-5bcc-876d-3ca4d60883c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-108273" :question/problem "![](images/69/69217ab9fc86fdbb424a0716f00596197b7da9e930c74b12b82406ce2ee8354c.png)\n\nFor which of the following values of $x$ does the function $y=f(x),$ shown above, attain a local minimum?\n\nI. $x=-2$\n\nII. $x=2$\n\nIII. $x=4$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given points in turn.\n\n- The function does *not* have a local minimum at $x=-2.$ For any open interval containing $x=-2,$ there will be points on the graph that lie below $f(-2).$\n\n- The function has a local minimum at $x=2.$ For example, in the interval $(1.5,2.5),$ the smallest value of the function is attained at $x=2,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/8e/8e56e79caf5c7903de977ee66894961b6068e3974ffd9ff4482b2086fad290ef.png)\n\nWe can write this mathematically as $f(x)\\ge f(2)\\,$ for all $\\,x\\in (1.5,2.5).$\n\n- The function does *not* have a local minimum at $x=4.$ For any open interval containing $x=4,$ there will be points on the graph that lie below $f(4).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ba3d283d-aec7-5ac6-8a37-e60669f500c1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ba3d283d-aec7-5ac6-8a37-e60669f500c1" :answer-field/id #uuid "ba3d283d-aec7-5ac6-8a37-e60669f500c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5b537279-77cf-5ca5-9ac7-685733e882ab" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2c8efd42-1acd-518a-acd7-fd5114b2fc66" "answer-d370875e-ff33-5ebf-b027-ccdba1d1d6f2" "answer-02383d20-0e98-5f57-be6b-6df75947ede2" "answer-5b537279-77cf-5ca5-9ac7-685733e882ab" "answer-2f1bfff1-53da-50a6-bf4c-4a078bd1dc97"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2c8efd42-1acd-518a-acd7-fd5114b2fc66" :answer/id #uuid "2c8efd42-1acd-518a-acd7-fd5114b2fc66" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d370875e-ff33-5ebf-b027-ccdba1d1d6f2" :answer/id #uuid "d370875e-ff33-5ebf-b027-ccdba1d1d6f2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-02383d20-0e98-5f57-be6b-6df75947ede2" :answer/id #uuid "02383d20-0e98-5f57-be6b-6df75947ede2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5b537279-77cf-5ca5-9ac7-685733e882ab" :answer/id #uuid "5b537279-77cf-5ca5-9ac7-685733e882ab" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2f1bfff1-53da-50a6-bf4c-4a078bd1dc97" :answer/id #uuid "2f1bfff1-53da-50a6-bf4c-4a078bd1dc97" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a017d206-18fc-5343-8dce-b59fb9d7e098" :question/id #uuid "a017d206-18fc-5343-8dce-b59fb9d7e098" :question/math-academy-id "q-108281" :question/problem "![](images/b3/b39c4e174ccb11a533586b57f26958a8ebfb5de0f0920a86297653471862fa7f.png)\n\nLet $f(x)$ be the function shown above. Which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. $f(x)$ attains a local minimum at $x=-3$\n\nII. $f(x)$ attains a local maximum at $x=0$\n\nIII. $f(x)$ attains a local maximum at $x=3$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function has a local minimum at $x=-3.$ For example, in the interval $(-3.5,-2.5),$ the smallest value of the function is attained at $x=-3,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/be/be3b86690beb961d54380b72736d945af3d735410da3bfe34cfb2cdead859f81.png)\n\nWe can write this mathematically as $f(x)\\ge f(-3)\\,$ for all $\\,x\\in (-3.5,-2.5).$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function has a local maximum at $x=0.$ For example, in the interval $(-0.5,0.5),$ the greatest value of the function is attained at $x=0,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/43/439117bd94dbc147d3cd2de49abd45c4b4790251ce25d1a79ecb31e03081068c.png)\n\nWe can write this mathematically as $f(x)\\le f(0)\\,$ for all $\\,x\\in (-0.5,0.5).$\n\n- Statement III is false. The function has a local minimum at $x=3.$ For example, in the interval $(2.75,3.25),$ the smallest value of the function is attained at $x=3,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/33/33aee4724507cff3e9ca87c78aa77c0ecb0df5f59a7bcf77359c1ca4e957f73c.png)\n\nWe can write this mathematically as $f(3)\\le f(x)\\,$ for all $\\,x\\in (2.75,3.25).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0dcd1d5b-00e0-5387-8447-4e14b932e6e4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0dcd1d5b-00e0-5387-8447-4e14b932e6e4" :answer-field/id #uuid "0dcd1d5b-00e0-5387-8447-4e14b932e6e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-601fb171-f90d-56af-8f89-4f19d2e3bf2d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-601fb171-f90d-56af-8f89-4f19d2e3bf2d" "answer-caaed8ed-6629-529d-acc1-d987c3b66548" "answer-fa2cf7e8-ae79-5fbe-b6e9-d01dfef172ce" "answer-527d4204-19b0-5174-b400-5eadfbe3a358" "answer-2e11ac1e-4cac-5e4b-be57-7818261b4df3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-601fb171-f90d-56af-8f89-4f19d2e3bf2d" :answer/id #uuid "601fb171-f90d-56af-8f89-4f19d2e3bf2d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-caaed8ed-6629-529d-acc1-d987c3b66548" :answer/id #uuid "caaed8ed-6629-529d-acc1-d987c3b66548" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fa2cf7e8-ae79-5fbe-b6e9-d01dfef172ce" :answer/id #uuid "fa2cf7e8-ae79-5fbe-b6e9-d01dfef172ce" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-527d4204-19b0-5174-b400-5eadfbe3a358" :answer/id #uuid "527d4204-19b0-5174-b400-5eadfbe3a358" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2e11ac1e-4cac-5e4b-be57-7818261b4df3" :answer/id #uuid "2e11ac1e-4cac-5e4b-be57-7818261b4df3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-50fb8efe-0b0b-50a4-80a5-fe5eb2cee4f9" :question/id #uuid "50fb8efe-0b0b-50a4-80a5-fe5eb2cee4f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-108293" :question/problem "![](images/b0/b00882caec033394ff22a01df1f8cc417a8336b475962e73662316c3091229fa.png)\n\nLet $f(x)$ be the function given by the graph above. Which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. $f(x)$ attains a local maximum at $x=-2$\n\nII. $f(x)$ attains a local minimum at $x=1$\n\nIII. $f(x)$ attains a local minimum at $x=4$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function has a local maximum at $x=-2.$ For example, in the interval $(-2.5,-1.5),$ the greatest value of the function is attained at $x=-2,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/ed/ed1ad21056cf9c05c621180126387f2e07a1cd349f151726142fb33d2b514e44.png)\n\nWe can write this mathematically as $f(x)\\le f(-2)\\,$ for all $\\,x\\in (-2.5,-1.5).$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function has a local minimum at $x=1.$ For example, in the interval $(0.5,1.5),$ the value of the function at $x=1$ is the same as the values of the function at the other points of the interval, as shown below. \n\n![](images/ff/ff8960289497889b1fb046c05b0bb2243a8776c435bb685e3c94fb7fe832e056.png)\n\nWe can write this mathematically as $f(x)\\ge f(1)\\,$ for all $\\,x\\in (0.5,1.5).$\n\n- Statement III is false. The function has a local maximum at $x=4.$ For example, in the interval $(3.75,4.25),$ the greatest value of the function is attained at $x=4,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/b2/b2d1f1b6b065c5d3511ec02350eae1a867b9473fa2ac7c1a1cb9458d1421fe03.png)\n\nWe can write this mathematically as $f(x)\\le f(4)\\,$ for all $\\,x\\in (3.75,4.25).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a58b8344-1eff-5105-8f43-9022f2a12252"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a58b8344-1eff-5105-8f43-9022f2a12252" :answer-field/id #uuid "a58b8344-1eff-5105-8f43-9022f2a12252" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-813deb3f-953d-56d5-a179-599290773b95" :answer-field/choices ["answer-813deb3f-953d-56d5-a179-599290773b95" "answer-2e576dfa-b2f3-54c2-a027-1f67463d58c8" "answer-b1e68446-2fd6-56de-b8a8-731047d771c6" "answer-e6872077-c939-529a-88df-fbe164e0d487" "answer-3920d518-8437-576a-b180-614b1dc2ef2b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-813deb3f-953d-56d5-a179-599290773b95" :answer/id #uuid "813deb3f-953d-56d5-a179-599290773b95" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2e576dfa-b2f3-54c2-a027-1f67463d58c8" :answer/id #uuid "2e576dfa-b2f3-54c2-a027-1f67463d58c8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b1e68446-2fd6-56de-b8a8-731047d771c6" :answer/id #uuid "b1e68446-2fd6-56de-b8a8-731047d771c6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e6872077-c939-529a-88df-fbe164e0d487" :answer/id #uuid "e6872077-c939-529a-88df-fbe164e0d487" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3920d518-8437-576a-b180-614b1dc2ef2b" :answer/id #uuid "3920d518-8437-576a-b180-614b1dc2ef2b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e60840d8-8ef2-5027-842a-3130fbad5883" :question/id #uuid "e60840d8-8ef2-5027-842a-3130fbad5883" :question/math-academy-id "q-90634" :question/problem "![](images/d1/d13474a62324af5edcaafd10e60c6b02c257f32c015d5769512725d157e01420.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $f(x)$ attains a **global** maximum at $x=4$\n\nII. $f(x)$ attains a local maximum at $x=2$\n\nIII. $f(x)$ attains a **global** minimum at $x=5$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The function does *not* have a global maximum at $x=4.$ The global maximum is the highest point on the graph, but our function has points where its value is greater than $f(4).$ For example, $f(7)>f(4).$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function has a local maximum at $x=2.$ For example, in the interval $(1.5,2.5),$ the greatest value of the function is attained at $x=2,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/32/32c866706140c0bc9a40bcb0ffdca4a827578e2d16dbcefc2cdb66b5c05b1c1c.png)\n\n \nWe can write this mathematically as $f(x)\\le f(2)\\,$ for all $\\,x\\in (1.5,2.5).$\n\n- Statement III is false. The function does *not* have a global minimum at $x=5.$ The global minimum is the lowest point on the graph, but our function has points where its value is smaller than $f(5).$ For example, $f(0)<f(5).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-cf7eaa68-885c-514e-a7ed-820109c92b1a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-cf7eaa68-885c-514e-a7ed-820109c92b1a" :answer-field/id #uuid "cf7eaa68-885c-514e-a7ed-820109c92b1a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7d044135-ffc0-53de-a8ed-edcab8fbf246" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f0731edc-4cd5-5128-a230-3aa0e3e14b08" "answer-7ae0b263-c07e-5e3f-ad0d-015a4284eb98" "answer-79e94892-8258-5ef2-824f-178abd4ba976" "answer-8bc7c11b-5e49-5add-a829-e5b05338f3ea" "answer-7d044135-ffc0-53de-a8ed-edcab8fbf246"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f0731edc-4cd5-5128-a230-3aa0e3e14b08" :answer/id #uuid "f0731edc-4cd5-5128-a230-3aa0e3e14b08" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7ae0b263-c07e-5e3f-ad0d-015a4284eb98" :answer/id #uuid "7ae0b263-c07e-5e3f-ad0d-015a4284eb98" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-79e94892-8258-5ef2-824f-178abd4ba976" :answer/id #uuid "79e94892-8258-5ef2-824f-178abd4ba976" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8bc7c11b-5e49-5add-a829-e5b05338f3ea" :answer/id #uuid "8bc7c11b-5e49-5add-a829-e5b05338f3ea" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7d044135-ffc0-53de-a8ed-edcab8fbf246" :answer/id #uuid "7d044135-ffc0-53de-a8ed-edcab8fbf246" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f8983cc1-bd12-59ce-89e8-0458cbfae2b2" :question/id #uuid "f8983cc1-bd12-59ce-89e8-0458cbfae2b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-90637" :question/problem "![](images/8e/8e0125007c4384cdd3eb5d939176b2d1ede7a6525da26fb5e99ccfe67e4077a9.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $f(x)$ attains a **global** maximum at $x=4$\n\nII. $f(x)$ attains a local minimum at $x=3$\n\nIII. $f(x)$ attains a **global** maximum at $x=1$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the function has a global maximum at $x=4.$ The global maximum is the highest point on the graph and our function does *not* have points where its value is greater than $f(4).$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function has a local minimum at $x=3.$ For example, in the interval $(2.5,3.5),$ the smallest value of the function is attained at $x=3,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/c2/c288ece1ca29e5da8ec8d64583b25f7538a929a3de295c24be6396339649d2d4.png)\n\n \nWe can write this mathematically as $f(x)\\ge f(3)\\,$ for all $\\,x\\in (2.5,3.5).$\n\n- Statement III is false. The function does *not* have a global maximum at $x=1.$ The global maximum is the highest point on the graph, but our function has points where its value is greatest than $f(1).$ For example, $f(4)>f(1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-50bdcfb3-d31d-58ba-ade3-e108e9937b5c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-50bdcfb3-d31d-58ba-ade3-e108e9937b5c" :answer-field/id #uuid "50bdcfb3-d31d-58ba-ade3-e108e9937b5c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-69d3c833-109c-55b6-b9de-758ec5a86527" :answer-field/choices ["answer-69d3c833-109c-55b6-b9de-758ec5a86527" "answer-92c3e3d1-b0c8-562f-8bcd-8d77d551c467" "answer-a79e3ac5-259e-5485-aea5-ad1f8c55e3cd" "answer-a6c18fdc-de58-518a-8778-1344568ab9bb" "answer-0f56b3cc-115f-57ad-a4f5-2b4f7ee5bbfb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-69d3c833-109c-55b6-b9de-758ec5a86527" :answer/id #uuid "69d3c833-109c-55b6-b9de-758ec5a86527" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-92c3e3d1-b0c8-562f-8bcd-8d77d551c467" :answer/id #uuid "92c3e3d1-b0c8-562f-8bcd-8d77d551c467" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a79e3ac5-259e-5485-aea5-ad1f8c55e3cd" :answer/id #uuid "a79e3ac5-259e-5485-aea5-ad1f8c55e3cd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a6c18fdc-de58-518a-8778-1344568ab9bb" :answer/id #uuid "a6c18fdc-de58-518a-8778-1344568ab9bb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0f56b3cc-115f-57ad-a4f5-2b4f7ee5bbfb" :answer/id #uuid "0f56b3cc-115f-57ad-a4f5-2b4f7ee5bbfb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f3f7c8b0-18ee-5d7c-bcd5-63f6de1aef9a" :question/id #uuid "f3f7c8b0-18ee-5d7c-bcd5-63f6de1aef9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-108296" :question/problem "![](images/e0/e0b8b8968b650e53d35766ede2b22aa187a7301f559d1687fa6bc630c1152701.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $f(x)$ attains a local minimum at $x=-2$\n\nII. $f(x)$ attains a **global** minimum at $x=1$\n\nIII. $f(x)$ attains a local maximum at $x=3$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function has a local minimum at $x=-2.$ For example, in the interval $(-2.5,-1.5),$ the smallest value of the function is attained at $x=-2,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/f7/f7abc15ac37d26d6daea9855cdebd2ef0ae5e27a27d65480b51da3cb7b19a395.png)\n\n \nWe can write this mathematically as $f(x)\\ge f(-2)\\,$ for all $\\,x\\in (-2.5,-1.5).$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the function has a global minimum at $x=1.$ The global minimum is the lowest point on the graph and our function does not have points where its value is smaller than $f(1).$\n\n- Statement III is true. The function has a local maximum at $x=3.$ For example, in the interval $(2.5,3.5),$ the greatest value of the function is attained at $x=3,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/19/19d3370f7113e6ebfaa2d1f53a3d097de796e6d3413f06414d35e6a7ade9b9b6.png)\n\n \nWe can write this mathematically as $f(x)\\le f(3)\\,$ for all $\\,x\\in (2.5,3.5).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1a5d9368-bd94-5aac-9c74-f801a63761ce"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1a5d9368-bd94-5aac-9c74-f801a63761ce" :answer-field/id #uuid "1a5d9368-bd94-5aac-9c74-f801a63761ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-cd6d6a7c-e4ba-5e33-ad17-2e914a1c904b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-58b3937e-8748-534e-8f8a-8a78179a3ca0" "answer-cd6d6a7c-e4ba-5e33-ad17-2e914a1c904b" "answer-f922dea5-ab43-5da8-94b1-11fd66e881c6" "answer-3e2ebe3b-5917-5d4e-b709-6ae7dc69f409" "answer-8b1acef0-82ab-5835-b335-a0df696d5895"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-58b3937e-8748-534e-8f8a-8a78179a3ca0" :answer/id #uuid "58b3937e-8748-534e-8f8a-8a78179a3ca0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cd6d6a7c-e4ba-5e33-ad17-2e914a1c904b" :answer/id #uuid "cd6d6a7c-e4ba-5e33-ad17-2e914a1c904b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f922dea5-ab43-5da8-94b1-11fd66e881c6" :answer/id #uuid "f922dea5-ab43-5da8-94b1-11fd66e881c6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3e2ebe3b-5917-5d4e-b709-6ae7dc69f409" :answer/id #uuid "3e2ebe3b-5917-5d4e-b709-6ae7dc69f409" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8b1acef0-82ab-5835-b335-a0df696d5895" :answer/id #uuid "8b1acef0-82ab-5835-b335-a0df696d5895" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186153362 :tutorial/content "A **local extremum** (or **relative extremum**) of a function is a point that is either a maximum or minimum point of the function relative to an (open) interval in the function's domain. \n\nFor example, consider the function shown in the graph below. \n\n![](images/19/197834de138cb2e05a3af391bbeec1279ad8bfddfb277b881e5e3eeb49bcb73a.png)\n\nThe graph has a peak at the point $A$ where $x=0.$ We say that the function has a **local maximum** when $x=0.$ We also say that the point $A$ is a **local maximum point** of the function. \n\nThis means that we can take an open interval of $x$-values around $x=0$ such that $A$ will be the highest point on the graph in this interval. \n\n![](images/d5/d522b4b0a59ff328399493bc12a770af2d99a1587b2826accb9835d7463ef9ba.png)\n\nSimilarly, the graph has a valley at the point $B$ where $x=2.$ We say that the function has a **local minimum** when $x=2.$ We can also say that the point $B$ is a **local minimum point** of the function.\n\nThis means that we can take an open interval of $x$-values around $x=2$ such that $B$ will be the lowest point on the graph in this interval. \n\n![](images/e2/e2a92d4217e1eebcc724e48cca273d2e707731ded914207e59fff149b46b4dcc.png)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186153407 :tutorial/content "Let's go back to looking at the function $y=f(x)$ that we saw earlier.\n\n![](images/19/197834de138cb2e05a3af391bbeec1279ad8bfddfb277b881e5e3eeb49bcb73a.png)\n\nThe point $A$ where $x=0$ is a local maximum of $f(x)$ because we can draw an open interval around $x=0$ such that the point $A$ is the highest point on the graph. One such interval is $x\\in (-0.5,0.5),$ shown below:\n\n![](images/d5/d522b4b0a59ff328399493bc12a770af2d99a1587b2826accb9835d7463ef9ba.png)\n\nEverywhere in this interval, the value of the function is *less than or equal to* $f(0),$ the largest value of the function in this interval. We can write this mathematically as\n\n$\\qquad$ $f(x) \\leq f(0)$ for all $x\\in(-0.5, 0.5).$\n\nSimilarly, the point $B$ where $x=2$ is a local minimum of $f(x)$ because we can draw an open interval around $x=2$ such that the point $B$ is the lowest point on the graph. One such interval is $x\\in (1.5,2.5),$ shown below:\n\n![](images/e2/e2a92d4217e1eebcc724e48cca273d2e707731ded914207e59fff149b46b4dcc.png)\n\nEverywhere in this interval, the value of the function is *greater than or equal to* $f(2),$ the smallest value of the function in this interval. We can write this mathematically as\n\n$\\qquad$ $f(x) \\geq f(2)$ for all $x\\in(1.5, 2.5).$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186153406 :tutorial/content "Can a point on a graph be both a local minimum *and* a local maximum?\n\nTo answer this question, let's consider the function $f(x)$ given by the graph below. \n\n![](images/0c/0cec91d9ec8e108ee4836e816aae63722fc4c09d997790eaf283cd3e2a9c4bc6.png)\n\nNow consider the point where $x=2.$ Our goal is to determine which type of extremum it is. To do this, we consider the values of $f(x)$ in the open interval $(1.5, 2.5),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/c6/c6cf1a6237c50d5c2b651937ca46b9ae52a08dafb831d76b27ddb068d37dd501.png)\n\nNow, notice the following:\n\n- The point where $x=2$ is a local maximum because $f(x) \\le f(2) \\:$ for all $\\: x \\in (1.5,2.5)\\quad {\\color{\\green}\\checkmark}$\n\n- The point where $x=2$ is a local minimum because $f(x) \\ge f(2) \\:$ for all $\\: x \\in (1.5,2.5)\\quad {\\color{\\green}\\checkmark}$\n\nTherefore, $x=2$ is both a local maximum *and* a local minimum of $f(x)!$ \n\nThis might seem strange. But, notice that $x=2$ lies within an interval where the function is constant. This is a special case where a particular point is both a local minimum and a local maximum." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152350 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186326906 :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/26/264cb888010525848f647669653d34383535dd32e6ac264927105540c3b85f9f.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=x$ and $Q(x,y)=-(x-y).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):\\pi \\le x\\le 2\\pi ,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\le y\\le 0\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the Green's Theorem we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}xdx-(x-y)dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}^{0}dy\\,dx.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186326911 :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/73/73bf9b973e50c540a873f4f1a44d936474e4c49d7041a330902508a840645009.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}({x}^{3}+2{y}^{2})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{3}+2{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=4y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):y\\le x\\le 2-y,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({x}^{3}+2{y}^{2})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x-4y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{2-y}(2x-4y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{x}^{2}-4xy]}_{y}^{2-y}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[(2-y)^{2}-4(2-y)y-{y}^{2}+4{y}^{2}]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(8{y}^{2}-12y+4)\\,dy \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2{y}^{2}-3y+1)\\,dy \\\\  & =4{[\\frac{2{y}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{2}+y]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =4[\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{3}{2}+1] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186134074 :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/7c/7ca4b6fe3a5a16c4004c75b0e4524cc3b42550cb2413d5b8e1c21a1d53cee902.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={e}^{x}-2{x}^{2}y$ and $Q(x,y)={e}^{y}+2x{y}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-2{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =2{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-y\\le x\\le y,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({e}^{x}-2{x}^{2}y)\\,dx+({e}^{y}+2x{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2{y}^{2}+2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-y}^{y}(2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+x{y}^{2}]}_{-y}^{y}\\,dy \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}+{y}^{3}+\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}+{y}^{3})dy \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{8{y}^{3}}{3}\\,dy \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{3}\\,dy \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}{[{y}^{4}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot {2}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186244257 :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/e3/e32b8e7d6ce80d7cedad928f9a2f7acc3c3d7f06add7909e856db27d154068d6.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+x)-{y}^{2})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+x)-{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 2,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le x+2\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)-{y}^{2})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x+2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{x+2}2(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "activity-9825eb55-72d5-5b07-8e8a-c069c488575b" :activity/id #uuid "9825eb55-72d5-5b07-8e8a-c069c488575b" :activity/title "Introduction to Green's Theorem" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186051070 :activity/course 17592186046660 :activity/steps ["step-0ae01de5-d64a-538a-80c0-7da40c88a92f" "step-fef008b4-0303-5e84-9af0-986223a72296" "step-f22dc590-f6dc-5c55-9547-5f97dd757472" "step-a6ba7b18-0939-5bd9-b0f6-82c7785070e6" "step-8016382a-6b8c-54d4-9839-9c63e2a7f47d"] :activity/first-step "step-0ae01de5-d64a-538a-80c0-7da40c88a92f" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-0ae01de5-d64a-538a-80c0-7da40c88a92f" :step/id #uuid "0ae01de5-d64a-538a-80c0-7da40c88a92f" :step/content 17592186326906 :step/next "step-fef008b4-0303-5e84-9af0-986223a72296" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-fef008b4-0303-5e84-9af0-986223a72296" :step/id #uuid "fef008b4-0303-5e84-9af0-986223a72296" :step/content 17592186134367 :step/next "step-f22dc590-f6dc-5c55-9547-5f97dd757472" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-f22dc590-f6dc-5c55-9547-5f97dd757472" :step/id #uuid "f22dc590-f6dc-5c55-9547-5f97dd757472" :step/content 17592186326911 :step/next "step-a6ba7b18-0939-5bd9-b0f6-82c7785070e6" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-a6ba7b18-0939-5bd9-b0f6-82c7785070e6" :step/id #uuid "a6ba7b18-0939-5bd9-b0f6-82c7785070e6" :step/content 17592186134074 :step/next "step-8016382a-6b8c-54d4-9839-9c63e2a7f47d" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-8016382a-6b8c-54d4-9839-9c63e2a7f47d" :step/id #uuid "8016382a-6b8c-54d4-9839-9c63e2a7f47d" :step/content 17592186244257 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186104050 :answer-field/choices 17592186104051] [:db/retract 17592186104050 :answer-field/choices 17592186104052] [:db/retract 17592186104050 :answer-field/choices 17592186104053] [:db/retract 17592186104050 :answer-field/choices 17592186104054] [:db/retract 17592186104050 :answer-field/choices 17592186104055] {:db/id 17592186105718 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-8d216a5f-ca4b-530d-81c3-76f09a214af9"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186106317 :question/problem "![](images/48/48d9d8513f45b87f9deffeffcef31e49a6dcaba0b033c91a918c1d72d87eb215.png)\n\nIn the quadrilateral $ABCD$ above, determine the measure of $∠C.$" :question/worked-solution "A quadrilateral has $n=4$ sides. Therefore, the sum of the interior angles of a quadrilateral  must be \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2) & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot (4-2) \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding up the internal angles and solving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A+m∠B+m∠C+m∠D & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ x+{90}^{∘}+3x+6x & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ 10x+{90}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ 10x & ={270}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={27}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠C & =3x \\\\  & =3({27}^{∘}) \\\\  & ={81}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186104050 :answer-field/correct "answer-55f686c3-4dc7-5ee6-94df-392ace9f44b3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b20abdd4-a291-58d8-b13c-b588f3c268d0" "answer-a4ab1399-f698-5779-856e-01b204aa3767" "answer-b25b8a4a-c870-5c94-9647-8973446f4945" "answer-0dc54541-63d3-5382-8d61-ad8fb7a2df0c" "answer-55f686c3-4dc7-5ee6-94df-392ace9f44b3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b20abdd4-a291-58d8-b13c-b588f3c268d0" :answer/id #uuid "b20abdd4-a291-58d8-b13c-b588f3c268d0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{34}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a4ab1399-f698-5779-856e-01b204aa3767" :answer/id #uuid "a4ab1399-f698-5779-856e-01b204aa3767" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{63}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b25b8a4a-c870-5c94-9647-8973446f4945" :answer/id #uuid "b25b8a4a-c870-5c94-9647-8973446f4945" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{68}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0dc54541-63d3-5382-8d61-ad8fb7a2df0c" :answer/id #uuid "0dc54541-63d3-5382-8d61-ad8fb7a2df0c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{75}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-55f686c3-4dc7-5ee6-94df-392ace9f44b3" :answer/id #uuid "55f686c3-4dc7-5ee6-94df-392ace9f44b3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{81}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-8d216a5f-ca4b-530d-81c3-76f09a214af9" :question/id #uuid "8d216a5f-ca4b-530d-81c3-76f09a214af9" :question/math-academy-id "q-4316" :question/problem "![](images/fa/fa797de4c7edfb4e4c4bc4eab40e8443f832c0407f08fd9d9ae28730c468a71e.png)\n\nIn the quadrilateral $OMNP$ shown above, find $x.$" :question/worked-solution "A quadrilateral has $n=4$ sides. Therefore, the sum of the interior angles of a quadrilateral must be \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2) & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot (4-2) \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding up the internal angles and solving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠O+m∠M+m∠N+m∠P & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ {93}^{∘}+(14x-{7}^{∘})+(11x-{2}^{∘})+{76}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ 25x+{160}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ 25x & ={200}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={8}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-172b1f55-032f-506f-a1f3-de37620daea9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-172b1f55-032f-506f-a1f3-de37620daea9" :answer-field/id #uuid "172b1f55-032f-506f-a1f3-de37620daea9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-779f08b8-d128-5006-babf-2a4ce92fda54" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8845496d-a3e1-5e36-8bf1-e36d44baa191" "answer-78305b75-af32-5483-8ec7-da87251e1870" "answer-c14baac9-826a-5be1-a8d3-292463298b03" "answer-23ba891b-8c25-5525-b032-ab5b189de223" "answer-779f08b8-d128-5006-babf-2a4ce92fda54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8845496d-a3e1-5e36-8bf1-e36d44baa191" :answer/id #uuid "8845496d-a3e1-5e36-8bf1-e36d44baa191" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{10}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-78305b75-af32-5483-8ec7-da87251e1870" :answer/id #uuid "78305b75-af32-5483-8ec7-da87251e1870" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{14}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c14baac9-826a-5be1-a8d3-292463298b03" :answer/id #uuid "c14baac9-826a-5be1-a8d3-292463298b03" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{21}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-23ba891b-8c25-5525-b032-ab5b189de223" :answer/id #uuid "23ba891b-8c25-5525-b032-ab5b189de223" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{3}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-779f08b8-d128-5006-babf-2a4ce92fda54" :answer/id #uuid "779f08b8-d128-5006-babf-2a4ce92fda54" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{8}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105719 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-918f9202-d934-535a-b14f-29131ec63f3f"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-918f9202-d934-535a-b14f-29131ec63f3f" :question/id #uuid "918f9202-d934-535a-b14f-29131ec63f3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-118069" :question/problem "The sum of the interior angles of a polygon is ${3960}^{∘}.$ How many sides does it have?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the sum of the interior angles of a polygon with $n$ sides is ${180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2).$\n\nTo find the number of sides in our polygon, we start by setting up the following equation:\n\n$$\n{180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2)=3\\,{960}^{∘}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $n,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2) & =3\\,{960}^{∘} \\\\ n-2 & =\\frac{3\\,{960}^{∘}}{{180}^{∘}} \\\\ n-2 & =22 \\\\ n & =24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polygon has $n=24$ sides." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d8f0acf3-60c9-5fe9-a760-cefa37eb9b66"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d8f0acf3-60c9-5fe9-a760-cefa37eb9b66" :answer-field/id #uuid "d8f0acf3-60c9-5fe9-a760-cefa37eb9b66" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ca5c0925-8ebb-5ac7-b69a-43148347d1bf" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6f8c4f4d-a7cb-5f36-ba29-a840d9bbe587" "answer-ef70b68e-e007-52ca-93c9-efe7e0c22ece" "answer-ca5c0925-8ebb-5ac7-b69a-431483