5b" :step/content "question-d3857009-5cce-53bf-a9fe-94834655a415" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157305 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-13a87bfd-ec89-5321-9298-2cba42bc0bbc" "question-727c8803-de94-5b7f-a91c-cc70ef9900c0"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157303 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-8ba3cd0d-7f42-50d0-93f6-108a716173e3" "question-4e648a6d-37fa-5da0-b09f-42f9d00baf66"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-8ba3cd0d-7f42-50d0-93f6-108a716173e3" :question/id #uuid "8ba3cd0d-7f42-50d0-93f6-108a716173e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-84826" :question/problem "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(-3,-2)$ and which passes through the point $(2,3)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=2pt,\\,y-k=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(-3,-2),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx+3=2pt,\\,y+2=p{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(2,3),$ we can substitute $x=2$ and $y=3$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}5=2pt \\\\ 5=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =2pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{5}{2p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =p{(\\frac{5}{2p})}^{2} \\\\ 5 & =p\\cdot \\frac{25}{4{p}^{2}} \\\\ 5 & =\\frac{25}{4p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx+3=2(\\frac{5}{4})t,\\,y+2=(\\frac{5}{4}){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx+3=\\frac{5}{2}t,\\,y+2=\\frac{5}{4}{t}^{2}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-46647a5c-2b95-53fe-940a-e6b35e9ddd8e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-46647a5c-2b95-53fe-940a-e6b35e9ddd8e" :answer-field/id #uuid "46647a5c-2b95-53fe-940a-e6b35e9ddd8e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-481610f4-96eb-5391-8fa0-5b8e3917e1a4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-481610f4-96eb-5391-8fa0-5b8e3917e1a4" "answer-606af933-7d01-5aba-a01d-1698e07eb2f5" "answer-5fd94bab-ba3d-53e6-b7a3-be9c9c7e8fa8" "answer-e06459cd-3712-586f-bc68-69909dacd4dd" "answer-9bd7ca21-f69d-5100-a76d-a62b09fb4809"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-481610f4-96eb-5391-8fa0-5b8e3917e1a4" :answer/id #uuid "481610f4-96eb-5391-8fa0-5b8e3917e1a4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x+3=\\frac{5}{2}t,\\,y+2=\\frac{5}{4}{t}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-606af933-7d01-5aba-a01d-1698e07eb2f5" :answer/id #uuid "606af933-7d01-5aba-a01d-1698e07eb2f5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x+3=\\frac{8}{5}t,\\,y+2=\\frac{4}{5}{t}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5fd94bab-ba3d-53e6-b7a3-be9c9c7e8fa8" :answer/id #uuid "5fd94bab-ba3d-53e6-b7a3-be9c9c7e8fa8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x-2=\\frac{5}{2}t,\\,y-3=\\frac{5}{4}{t}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e06459cd-3712-586f-bc68-69909dacd4dd" :answer/id #uuid "e06459cd-3712-586f-bc68-69909dacd4dd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x-2=\\frac{5}{4}t,\\,y-3=\\frac{5}{4}{t}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9bd7ca21-f69d-5100-a76d-a62b09fb4809" :answer/id #uuid "9bd7ca21-f69d-5100-a76d-a62b09fb4809" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x-3=\\frac{8}{5}t,\\,y-2=\\frac{4}{5}{t}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-4e648a6d-37fa-5da0-b09f-42f9d00baf66" :question/id #uuid "4e648a6d-37fa-5da0-b09f-42f9d00baf66" :question/math-academy-id "q-106451" :question/problem "![](images/96/96e1647f6069dee4219f8cb92e3fb4277396394c016b27ecd4de557f87257000.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(3,-2),$ and passes through the point $(7,-6).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=2pt,\\,y-k=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(3,-2),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx-3=2pt,\\,y+2=p{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(7,-6),$ we can substitute $x=7$ and $y=-6$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4=2pt \\\\ -4=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =2pt \\\\ 2 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{2}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4 & =p{(\\frac{2}{p})}^{2} \\\\ -4 & =p\\cdot \\frac{4}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ -4 & =\\frac{4}{p} \\\\ p & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-3=2(-1)t,\\,y+2=(-1){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-3=-2t,\\,y+2=-{t}^{2}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-829f2828-1700-5400-9439-0d028651d780"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-829f2828-1700-5400-9439-0d028651d780" :answer-field/id #uuid "829f2828-1700-5400-9439-0d028651d780" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c0ba1921-c107-5fe6-bfe7-38c56ef94a05" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9e778123-9ee0-54cc-9eb6-f27d0cb7ddbf" "answer-aa4dd6aa-918f-5eba-ab69-561c4e51cffe" "answer-1d828ed6-02a5-556a-bcf0-6de2c7929742" "answer-cbac0851-5569-524e-ac10-92470ba43d20" "answer-c0ba1921-c107-5fe6-bfe7-38c56ef94a05"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9e778123-9ee0-54cc-9eb6-f27d0cb7ddbf" :answer/id #uuid "9e778123-9ee0-54cc-9eb6-f27d0cb7ddbf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x+3=-2t,\\,\\,y+2=-{t}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aa4dd6aa-918f-5eba-ab69-561c4e51cffe" :answer/id #uuid "aa4dd6aa-918f-5eba-ab69-561c4e51cffe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x+3=-2t,\\,\\,y-2=-{t}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1d828ed6-02a5-556a-bcf0-6de2c7929742" :answer/id #uuid "1d828ed6-02a5-556a-bcf0-6de2c7929742" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x+3=-t,\\,\\,y+2=-2{t}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cbac0851-5569-524e-ac10-92470ba43d20" :answer/id #uuid "cbac0851-5569-524e-ac10-92470ba43d20" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x-3=-2t,\\,\\,y+2=-2{t}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c0ba1921-c107-5fe6-bfe7-38c56ef94a05" :answer/id #uuid "c0ba1921-c107-5fe6-bfe7-38c56ef94a05" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x-3=-2t,\\,\\,y+2=-{t}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-13a87bfd-ec89-5321-9298-2cba42bc0bbc" :question/id #uuid "13a87bfd-ec89-5321-9298-2cba42bc0bbc" :question/math-academy-id "q-106506" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx+1=2{t}^{2},\\,y+3=4t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =\\frac{x+1}{2},\\,t=\\frac{y+3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(\\frac{y+3}{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=\\frac{x+1}{2}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{16} & =\\frac{x+1}{2} \\\\ (y+3)^{2} & =16\\cdot (\\frac{x+1}{2}) \\\\ (y+3)^{2} & =8(x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-314ab3a0-11b2-5836-a9a6-779f4e2cfc86"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-314ab3a0-11b2-5836-a9a6-779f4e2cfc86" :answer-field/id #uuid "314ab3a0-11b2-5836-a9a6-779f4e2cfc86" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6e7f9396-d71d-5308-95cc-a34867a20ca6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7c5d3114-e499-590c-baf7-861605384f54" "answer-2e072a8a-4c4e-573b-8869-941c8567a11b" "answer-7a134ec0-3a05-56eb-ad2c-0e83ebd565ee" "answer-dc78eb36-9d33-50e7-9b6c-c918281f4ed5" "answer-6e7f9396-d71d-5308-95cc-a34867a20ca6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7c5d3114-e499-590c-baf7-861605384f54" :answer/id #uuid "7c5d3114-e499-590c-baf7-861605384f54" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(x+1)^{2}=4(y+3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2e072a8a-4c4e-573b-8869-941c8567a11b" :answer/id #uuid "2e072a8a-4c4e-573b-8869-941c8567a11b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(x+1)^{2}=8(y+3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7a134ec0-3a05-56eb-ad2c-0e83ebd565ee" :answer/id #uuid "7a134ec0-3a05-56eb-ad2c-0e83ebd565ee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(y+3)^{2}=-8(x+1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dc78eb36-9d33-50e7-9b6c-c918281f4ed5" :answer/id #uuid "dc78eb36-9d33-50e7-9b6c-c918281f4ed5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(y+3)^{2}=4(x+1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6e7f9396-d71d-5308-95cc-a34867a20ca6" :answer/id #uuid "6e7f9396-d71d-5308-95cc-a34867a20ca6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(y+3)^{2}=8(x+1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-727c8803-de94-5b7f-a91c-cc70ef9900c0" :question/id #uuid "727c8803-de94-5b7f-a91c-cc70ef9900c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-84849" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx-4=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y+1=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =2(x-4),\\,t=y+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =(y+1)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=2(x-4)$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+1)^{2} & =2(x-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-44ae7850-0329-5a60-9d70-6fd1640a128d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-44ae7850-0329-5a60-9d70-6fd1640a128d" :answer-field/id #uuid "44ae7850-0329-5a60-9d70-6fd1640a128d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-830c8b4f-dd15-5945-be2a-c16066461306" :answer-field/choices ["answer-830c8b4f-dd15-5945-be2a-c16066461306" "answer-c1fc5336-dfe4-5a79-95c6-2401457bbad9" "answer-81396d4d-0bd7-575b-b2e7-739740b6660c" "answer-bce99d0a-b922-5732-8a5d-54c719314068" "answer-761e2e34-2b73-575e-ad93-69b3ef7a4509"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-830c8b4f-dd15-5945-be2a-c16066461306" :answer/id #uuid "830c8b4f-dd15-5945-be2a-c16066461306" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(y+1)^{2}=2(x-4)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c1fc5336-dfe4-5a79-95c6-2401457bbad9" :answer/id #uuid "c1fc5336-dfe4-5a79-95c6-2401457bbad9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(y+1)^{2}=8(x-4)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-81396d4d-0bd7-575b-b2e7-739740b6660c" :answer/id #uuid "81396d4d-0bd7-575b-b2e7-739740b6660c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(y+1)^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}(x-4)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bce99d0a-b922-5732-8a5d-54c719314068" :answer/id #uuid "bce99d0a-b922-5732-8a5d-54c719314068" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(y-1)^{2}=2(x+4)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-761e2e34-2b73-575e-ad93-69b3ef7a4509" :answer/id #uuid "761e2e34-2b73-575e-ad93-69b3ef7a4509" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(y-1)^{2}=8(x+4)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105924 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e38cbfdc-40f4-5e23-81ff-d9532344f653" "question-86d71eff-689a-54e2-8c09-3fae339805fb"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-e38cbfdc-40f4-5e23-81ff-d9532344f653" :question/id #uuid "e38cbfdc-40f4-5e23-81ff-d9532344f653" :question/math-academy-id "q-118856" :question/problem "Find the total area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve $y=|x{|}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}$ and the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "We find the area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve and the $x$-axis by calculating the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }|x{|}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}.\n$$\n\nWe break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }|x{|}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}|x{|}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }|x{|}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWe start by calculating the first of these integrals:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}|x{|}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{x}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWe calculate the antiderivative using a substitution. Let $u=-{x}^{4}$,  then $\\text{d}u=-4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Hence, the antiderivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\int {e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{u}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}|x{|}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{x}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{x}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{(-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-{x}^{4}})|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{0}-{e}^{-{a}^{4}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand $|x{|}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}$ is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }|x{|}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }|x{|}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}|x{|}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }|x{|}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ae057d8d-edb0-54d6-852b-f3d8185d31f2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ae057d8d-edb0-54d6-852b-f3d8185d31f2" :answer-field/id #uuid "ae057d8d-edb0-54d6-852b-f3d8185d31f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e3396cf7-276b-5255-a8b9-c8f3c0ee558c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-61db2b55-0411-5c2c-8bcf-dbb5a9ead9bb" "answer-28d2be98-9a4d-5c88-abaa-0360571b0ab6" "answer-e3396cf7-276b-5255-a8b9-c8f3c0ee558c" "answer-7b7f8da8-6744-55ff-b218-c7923a9889bd" "answer-41c64781-3167-56a3-b3e8-fd75bb289c1a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-61db2b55-0411-5c2c-8bcf-dbb5a9ead9bb" :answer/id #uuid "61db2b55-0411-5c2c-8bcf-dbb5a9ead9bb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-28d2be98-9a4d-5c88-abaa-0360571b0ab6" :answer/id #uuid "28d2be98-9a4d-5c88-abaa-0360571b0ab6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e3396cf7-276b-5255-a8b9-c8f3c0ee558c" :answer/id #uuid "e3396cf7-276b-5255-a8b9-c8f3c0ee558c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7b7f8da8-6744-55ff-b218-c7923a9889bd" :answer/id #uuid "7b7f8da8-6744-55ff-b218-c7923a9889bd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-41c64781-3167-56a3-b3e8-fd75bb289c1a" :answer/id #uuid "41c64781-3167-56a3-b3e8-fd75bb289c1a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186320508 :answer-field/correct "answer-0a011c53-bc00-5286-8ae6-e77021b09c7c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0a011c53-bc00-5286-8ae6-e77021b09c7c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0a011c53-bc00-5286-8ae6-e77021b09c7c" :answer/id #uuid "0a011c53-bc00-5286-8ae6-e77021b09c7c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-86d71eff-689a-54e2-8c09-3fae339805fb" :question/id #uuid "86d71eff-689a-54e2-8c09-3fae339805fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-351589" :question/problem "![](images/f7/f78efc78b9f7cded50b29ba63855d81ff4e9ee8b7d12aa9673fac1cb7644e50a.png)\n\nFind the total area $A$ of the region bounded by the graph of the curve $y=5{e}^{-|x|+1}$ and the $x$-axis, as shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We find the area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve and the $x$-axis by calculating the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }5{e}^{-|x|+1}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }5{e}^{-|x|+1}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}5{e}^{-|x|+1}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }5{e}^{-|x|+1}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}5{e}^{-|x|+1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}5{e}^{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}5{e}^{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{x+1}{|}_{a}^{0}\\,) \\\\  & =5{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{0+1}-{e}^{a+1}) \\\\  & =5(e-0) \\\\  & =5e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand $5{e}^{-|x|+1}$ is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }5{e}^{-|x|+1}\\,\\text{d}x=5e.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }5{e}^{-|x|+1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}5{e}^{-|x|+1}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }5{e}^{-|x|+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5e+5e \\\\  & =10e.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ba5aae1a-3e45-5846-af9a-9b9e31b42dfe"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ba5aae1a-3e45-5846-af9a-9b9e31b42dfe" :answer-field/id #uuid "ba5aae1a-3e45-5846-af9a-9b9e31b42dfe" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-d04d6f59-7d03-5768-bf23-8c1a24199af9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d04d6f59-7d03-5768-bf23-8c1a24199af9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d04d6f59-7d03-5768-bf23-8c1a24199af9" :answer/id #uuid "d04d6f59-7d03-5768-bf23-8c1a24199af9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10e" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186320878 :question/worked-solution "We find the area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve and the $x$-axis by calculating the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}){|}_{a}^{0}\\,) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(0-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+\\frac{3\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186320362 :answer-field/correct "answer-d16a7ece-3c28-5cdc-b1c9-a82826a0c636" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d16a7ece-3c28-5cdc-b1c9-a82826a0c636"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d16a7ece-3c28-5cdc-b1c9-a82826a0c636" :answer/id #uuid "d16a7ece-3c28-5cdc-b1c9-a82826a0c636" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-eed14073-dc62-5f83-88ec-a63140fff982" :activity/id #uuid "eed14073-dc62-5f83-88ec-a63140fff982" :activity/title "Improper Integrals Over the Real Line" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048880 :activity/course 17592186046645 :activity/steps ["step-06d66274-5a32-576e-a52b-391edbc866d9" "step-386ac35b-932b-5c6b-b512-8af34540aa39" "step-0b341115-0cca-598f-b097-bbbf9d6cbc06" "step-1d167963-e125-5607-90e6-742e213a968e" "step-807ac06a-e2d7-58a6-a284-e84be5d574ae"] :activity/first-step "step-06d66274-5a32-576e-a52b-391edbc866d9" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-06d66274-5a32-576e-a52b-391edbc866d9" :step/id #uuid "06d66274-5a32-576e-a52b-391edbc866d9" :step/content "question-e38cbfdc-40f4-5e23-81ff-d9532344f653" :step/next "step-386ac35b-932b-5c6b-b512-8af34540aa39" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-386ac35b-932b-5c6b-b512-8af34540aa39" :step/id #uuid "386ac35b-932b-5c6b-b512-8af34540aa39" :step/content 17592186319189 :step/next "step-0b341115-0cca-598f-b097-bbbf9d6cbc06" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-0b341115-0cca-598f-b097-bbbf9d6cbc06" :step/id #uuid "0b341115-0cca-598f-b097-bbbf9d6cbc06" :step/content 17592186320868 :step/next "step-1d167963-e125-5607-90e6-742e213a968e" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-1d167963-e125-5607-90e6-742e213a968e" :step/id #uuid "1d167963-e125-5607-90e6-742e213a968e" :step/content "question-86d71eff-689a-54e2-8c09-3fae339805fb" :step/next "step-807ac06a-e2d7-58a6-a284-e84be5d574ae" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-807ac06a-e2d7-58a6-a284-e84be5d574ae" :step/id #uuid "807ac06a-e2d7-58a6-a284-e84be5d574ae" :step/content 17592186320878 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166955 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-c0b2f698-58d0-5aff-9d1c-cd41dc57daac"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-c0b2f698-58d0-5aff-9d1c-cd41dc57daac" :question/id #uuid "c0b2f698-58d0-5aff-9d1c-cd41dc57daac" :question/math-academy-id "q-341226" :question/problem "Let $F(x)=x{e}^{3(x-1)}$ for $x\\in [0,5),$ and let $f(x)$ be the **odd** extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in (-5,5).$ What is the value of $f(-1)?$\n\n$f(-1)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [0,5),$ the odd extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}F(x), & x\\in [0,5), \\\\ -F(-x), & x\\in (-5,0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our cases, for $x\\in (-5,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =-F(-x) \\\\  & =-(-x){e}^{3(-x-1)} \\\\  & =x{e}^{3(-x-1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =(-1){e}^{3(-(-1)-1)}=-{e}^{3(0)}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-175c2fdb-c390-5385-bb9c-f23c3bd1dd27"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-175c2fdb-c390-5385-bb9c-f23c3bd1dd27" :answer-field/id #uuid "175c2fdb-c390-5385-bb9c-f23c3bd1dd27" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-19797644-81c9-5edc-8c9f-6d1f16ca9e89" :answer-field/choices ["answer-19797644-81c9-5edc-8c9f-6d1f16ca9e89"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-19797644-81c9-5edc-8c9f-6d1f16ca9e89" :answer/id #uuid "19797644-81c9-5edc-8c9f-6d1f16ca9e89" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186315064 :question/worked-solution "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [0,8),$ the odd extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}F(x), & x\\in [0,8), \\\\ -F(-x), & x\\in (-8,0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, for $x\\in (-8,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =-F(-x) \\\\  & =-(-(-x)^{2}+5(-x)) \\\\  & =-(-{x}^{2}-5x) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+5x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-3) & =(-3)^{2}+5(-3)=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186315030 :question/worked-solution "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ the odd extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}F(x), & x\\in [0,\\pi ), \\\\ -F(-x), & x\\in (-\\pi ,0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =-F(-x) \\\\  & =-(-2(-x)^{3}+3(-x)) \\\\  & =-(2{x}^{3}-3x) \\\\  & =-2{x}^{3}+3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186379589 :question/problem "$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=6x$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ The Fourier sine series of the **odd** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the expression for ${b}_{n}.$\n\n$\\,{b}_{n}=$ {{field-1}}" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186379575 :question/problem "$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=3$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ The Fourier sine series of the **odd** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the expression for ${b}_{n}.$\n\n$\\,{b}_{n}=$ {{field-1}}" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068695 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-1500b72e-c5d9-528b-baba-d8f0260732de" "question-dc42a1fb-c83b-51e0-b87b-3b70ebc495ac"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068696 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-29a769aa-7b05-577f-9b37-c2215185678e"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068549 :question/problem "![](images/18/1867a80955ae4b456cbcb011eefed96431f2b12926994942613eb947e55e38c6.png)\n\n \n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. $f(x)$ is defined at $x=2$\n\nII. $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\, 2}f(x)$ exists\n\nIII. $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-1500b72e-c5d9-528b-baba-d8f0260732de" :question/id #uuid "1500b72e-c5d9-528b-baba-d8f0260732de" :question/math-academy-id "q-69780" :question/problem "Select the function that is continuous at $x=-1.$" :question/worked-solution "Of the given options, the only one where we can draw the graph of the function without taking our pencil off of the paper in a neighborhood of $x=-1$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/ca/ca4279200e98b57570030ea6ecce9c04412645ff16d532f302cb13c6bf59c15e.png)\n\nAll other given options have breaks or asymptotes at $x=-1$, so it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=-1$ without taking our pencil off of the paper." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5a5dccb1-5f8c-5da2-bd89-36c396ed5b93"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5a5dccb1-5f8c-5da2-bd89-36c396ed5b93" :answer-field/id #uuid "5a5dccb1-5f8c-5da2-bd89-36c396ed5b93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-372e0c36-ea71-5545-9d96-c9a5701b7710" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e29524fa-4dfc-5780-91bc-0d34dd31acda" "answer-a2669f7c-0c1f-5912-9db0-dd5a2ad2b0d5" "answer-372e0c36-ea71-5545-9d96-c9a5701b7710" "answer-65d6fa45-91d4-557d-80d6-40b9833f56e5" "answer-468648d2-2ab6-54d7-bd55-da859a25ec39"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e29524fa-4dfc-5780-91bc-0d34dd31acda" :answer/id #uuid "e29524fa-4dfc-5780-91bc-0d34dd31acda" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/2a/2a384399032456cc37d3769746bf5cbeb12dfacac0bcc9c76d214a4d8424b9f2.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a2669f7c-0c1f-5912-9db0-dd5a2ad2b0d5" :answer/id #uuid "a2669f7c-0c1f-5912-9db0-dd5a2ad2b0d5" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/32/323242b8d2226dc4ce6b47bf038162135324ddab549213650b37bb57136fa689.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-372e0c36-ea71-5545-9d96-c9a5701b7710" :answer/id #uuid "372e0c36-ea71-5545-9d96-c9a5701b7710" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/3e/3e9b88b3538904a74157f632e7c89a3c1bb1f86264c8193a0d987525f36c47bf.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-65d6fa45-91d4-557d-80d6-40b9833f56e5" :answer/id #uuid "65d6fa45-91d4-557d-80d6-40b9833f56e5" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/4e/4e2a50b060d3f3042341269ccd2b323156292d52a65b347ed31c9a3c0746c047.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-468648d2-2ab6-54d7-bd55-da859a25ec39" :answer/id #uuid "468648d2-2ab6-54d7-bd55-da859a25ec39" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/74/749b6f597aad109a21602f7d9fb4bb0708d9b19b4d80e8234d69f0a5df14419e.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-dc42a1fb-c83b-51e0-b87b-3b70ebc495ac" :question/id #uuid "dc42a1fb-c83b-51e0-b87b-3b70ebc495ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-104016" :question/problem "![](images/9c/9c49ba7f220352a8933159a854d6e311baaaf2b6ec1e1f6fbdb78988653a157f.png)\n\nThe curve $y=f(x)$ is shown above. At which of the following points is $f(x)$ continuous?\n\nI. $x=-3$\n\nII. $x=0$\n\nIII. $x=2$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each point separately.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-3.$ Since there is an asymptote at $x=-3,$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=-3$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=0$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is also continuous at $x=2.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=2$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\nHence, the correct answer is  \"II and III only\".\n\n![](images/f9/f9c5342a872b4fd0ad2783b00d4d170b05844dc057a462832bf0eed41f50ccf4.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ab3af452-08a2-5a69-903f-a3fbbc0add5e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ab3af452-08a2-5a69-903f-a3fbbc0add5e" :answer-field/id #uuid "ab3af452-08a2-5a69-903f-a3fbbc0add5e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7d884a66-1aa4-58ca-8164-d58e6f2f707d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-62083efc-7529-5e30-8cd5-10a2ac34f827" "answer-9833b741-ee8c-5d09-8c5c-f6b3dd066d35" "answer-7d884a66-1aa4-58ca-8164-d58e6f2f707d" "answer-c0dbda4f-53b6-5cae-a016-7b51afc0e654" "answer-b33c3fcf-3dab-5d9d-9d7d-61353468e8e3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-62083efc-7529-5e30-8cd5-10a2ac34f827" :answer/id #uuid "62083efc-7529-5e30-8cd5-10a2ac34f827" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9833b741-ee8c-5d09-8c5c-f6b3dd066d35" :answer/id #uuid "9833b741-ee8c-5d09-8c5c-f6b3dd066d35" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7d884a66-1aa4-58ca-8164-d58e6f2f707d" :answer/id #uuid "7d884a66-1aa4-58ca-8164-d58e6f2f707d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c0dbda4f-53b6-5cae-a016-7b51afc0e654" :answer/id #uuid "c0dbda4f-53b6-5cae-a016-7b51afc0e654" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b33c3fcf-3dab-5d9d-9d7d-61353468e8e3" :answer/id #uuid "b33c3fcf-3dab-5d9d-9d7d-61353468e8e3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-29a769aa-7b05-577f-9b37-c2215185678e" :question/id #uuid "29a769aa-7b05-577f-9b37-c2215185678e" :question/math-academy-id "q-48089" :question/problem "![](images/17/17932f6186d98bb68ff231c07ae1e2b42dbe7c391ff442db6d4e8c9da92a9a82.png)\n\nWhat are the points of discontinuity of the function plotted above?" :question/worked-solution "The graph breaks at $x=2$, meaning that we can't draw the plot without taking our pencil off of the paper when passing through this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is  $x=2$ only." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-cf280544-fa9f-506b-ac2d-7fcf8a90b190"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-cf280544-fa9f-506b-ac2d-7fcf8a90b190" :answer-field/id #uuid "cf280544-fa9f-506b-ac2d-7fcf8a90b190" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a497a562-39c5-59e5-986e-1c6e833a2bc5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4a492d7e-03e3-53b9-b29f-5d23248be42a" "answer-ff301354-641f-5c50-8775-32e42d04d7ec" "answer-f7cc25a6-f144-5a0e-a8e0-dd12016dae8e" "answer-c3616842-989f-5138-afaa-77cd88a676d1" "answer-a497a562-39c5-59e5-986e-1c6e833a2bc5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4a492d7e-03e3-53b9-b29f-5d23248be42a" :answer/id #uuid "4a492d7e-03e3-53b9-b29f-5d23248be42a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=-2$ and $x=2$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ff301354-641f-5c50-8775-32e42d04d7ec" :answer/id #uuid "ff301354-641f-5c50-8775-32e42d04d7ec" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=-2$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f7cc25a6-f144-5a0e-a8e0-dd12016dae8e" :answer/id #uuid "f7cc25a6-f144-5a0e-a8e0-dd12016dae8e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=2$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c3616842-989f-5138-afaa-77cd88a676d1" :answer/id #uuid "c3616842-989f-5138-afaa-77cd88a676d1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a497a562-39c5-59e5-986e-1c6e833a2bc5" :answer/id #uuid "a497a562-39c5-59e5-986e-1c6e833a2bc5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186069958 :tutorial/content "A function $f(x)$ is **continuous** over its entire domain if the graph of $y=f(x)$ can be drawn using a pencil and a piece of paper without taking the pencil off of the paper.\n\nTo illustrate, let's consider the function $y=x^2$ whose graph is shown below. \n\n![](images/67/67ffd0b78845693b522100f4f0ba460cae6fedd796964b24708e320eab04ce29.png)\n\n \n\nWe can draw as much of the function $y=x^2$ as we want without taking our pencil off of the paper. Therefore, we say that $y=x^2$ is **continuous** at every point in its domain.\n\nLet's compare this to the function $y=g(x)$ below:\n\n![](images/a0/a07ccd15d14fa3eeb00a9c4ab07674c1c169e2a0d0a3e9fcb64798e9929e2ff9.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that this function has a jump in its value at $x=0.$\n\nIt is *impossible* to draw $y=g(x)$ without taking the pencil off the paper to fill in the jump at $x=0.$ Therefore, we say that $y=g(x)$ has a **discontinuity** at $x=0.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186069961 :tutorial/content "Now consider the function $y=h(x)$ below. This function has a discontinuity at $x=0,$ so it is not continuous over its entire domain.\n\n![](images/4e/4e41ca41e4befba33c62da68666a8c4a2e2c7a19c79331360ae4bf124edafd50.png)\n\n \n\nHowever, the function $h(x)$ may be continuous at other points, such as $x=1.$ \n\nTo determine whether $h(x)$ is continuous at $x=1,$ we have to consider points in the **neighborhood** of $x=1.$ Roughly speaking, the *neighborhood of $x=1$* means the points on the curve that have an $x$-coordinate that's close to $1.$\n\nIndeed, in the *neighborhood* of $x=1,$ we can draw the function $h(x)$ without taking the pencil off the paper. So, $h(x)$ is indeed continuous at $x=1.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068989 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162045 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-84533a55-9193-5a76-a277-23539897c843"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-84533a55-9193-5a76-a277-23539897c843" :question/id #uuid "84533a55-9193-5a76-a277-23539897c843" :question/math-academy-id "q-363663" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$  select the correct options in the computations of the matrix $U$ below.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce the singular values ${\\sigma }_{1}=3\\sqrt{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\n\nNow, we compute the $1$st column of $U.$ It is given by:\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${u}_{1}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |  |\n|  |  | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |  |  |\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal.\n\nHence, we must find vectors $u=[{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}]^{T}$ such that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to \n\n$$\n{{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nChoosing ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ as free variables, the general solution is given by:\n\n|  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | {{field-5}} |  |  |\n|  |  | $-2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}$ |  |  |\n\nthat we can rewrite as:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{1}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+{x}_{2}{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}}\n$$\n\nNext, we orthogonalize ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process to get the corresponding orthogonal vectors ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$\n\nLet ${c}_{1}={b}_{1},$ then:\n\n|  |  | {{field-6}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${c}_{2}=\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\,$ |  | $5$ |  |  |\n|  |  | {{field-7}} |  |  |\n\nFinally, normalizing orthogonal vectors ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ we obtain the second and third columns of $U:$   \n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"40px\">\n                ${u}_{2}=\\,\\,$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"90px\">\n                {{field-8}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n                {{field-9}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</td>\n<td>\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"40px\">\n                ${u}_{3}=\\,\\,$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"90px\">\n                {{field-10}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n                {{field-11}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=3\\sqrt{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\nAlso, the columns ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the first column of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find vectors \n\n$$\nu=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nsuch that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0$ and $‖u‖=1.$\n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{1}-\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,2{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get the general solution \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{1}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+{x}_{2}{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are free variables. \n\nNotice that the two linearly independent solutions ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ are *not* mutually orthogonal. Therefore, we have to orthogonalize them using the Gram-Schmidt process. So, let \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}={b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & ={b}_{2}-\\frac{{b}_{2}\\cdot {c}_{1}}{{c}_{1}\\cdot {c}_{1}}{c}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{(-4)}{5}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, normalizing orthogonal vectors ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ we obtain the second and third columns of $U:$   \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}],\\,{u}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{5}{3\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix $U$ could be the following:\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} & 0 & \\frac{5}{3\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f3a85cef-fa7c-5a3f-95d7-68572b164e28" "answer-field-6a1c4465-2c9b-5772-9efc-361ca67d4ae5" "answer-field-022729cb-d9f0-59e8-817f-cceaaef36b24" "answer-field-32e17515-8ee3-591d-b097-cc76971e56a6" "answer-field-5cc8af57-1e76-5dda-8c4c-b167f23eb7d1" "answer-field-76382f0f-4fac-5978-a7ef-f6f9979cc89e" "answer-field-612e6b5b-a086-50df-bca1-5f2a79f88051" "answer-field-da4b59a2-41bc-57f1-ab7b-fd67deaeecb8" "answer-field-df2e3317-5784-5bcd-a80a-10ea98305234" "answer-field-2fdd0d8d-363c-5219-9e9a-cca065aaec1f" "answer-field-9b024819-28c6-57b0-b677-48efa6e1b66f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f3a85cef-fa7c-5a3f-95d7-68572b164e28" :answer-field/id #uuid "f3a85cef-fa7c-5a3f-95d7-68572b164e28" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-52c6937e-fe66-516e-a4b1-fac71d99f3d1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-cecfcc4e-2ba5-5f52-b0ef-793f85fe11dd" "answer-4b2e59d6-51a7-5fe2-89d0-05e8293c8d47" "answer-52c6937e-fe66-516e-a4b1-fac71d99f3d1" "answer-71ccef85-acdf-5822-b7cd-1c90700e66a5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cecfcc4e-2ba5-5f52-b0ef-793f85fe11dd" :answer/id #uuid "cecfcc4e-2ba5-5f52-b0ef-793f85fe11dd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4b2e59d6-51a7-5fe2-89d0-05e8293c8d47" :answer/id #uuid "4b2e59d6-51a7-5fe2-89d0-05e8293c8d47" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-52c6937e-fe66-516e-a4b1-fac71d99f3d1" :answer/id #uuid "52c6937e-fe66-516e-a4b1-fac71d99f3d1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-71ccef85-acdf-5822-b7cd-1c90700e66a5" :answer/id #uuid "71ccef85-acdf-5822-b7cd-1c90700e66a5" :answer/type :answer.ty