ne the number of solutions, we can compute the discriminant. We have $a=4,$ $b=8,$ and $c=-12,$ so the discriminant is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(8)^{2}-4(4)(-12) \\\\  & =64+192 \\\\  & =256\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the discriminant is positive $(D>0),$ the equation has two distinct real solutions." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-355a3e72-0ca4-5416-b98c-d761ee9e21bb"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-355a3e72-0ca4-5416-b98c-d761ee9e21bb" :answer-field/id #uuid "355a3e72-0ca4-5416-b98c-d761ee9e21bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d7b129fd-cf43-5f11-9a88-c2df6b3b3d89" :answer-field/choices ["answer-46794791-4cb5-55d4-bbfa-4d50cbc19e9d" "answer-6c8a3e36-af4d-58f6-9536-c324e5353465" "answer-d7b129fd-cf43-5f11-9a88-c2df6b3b3d89" "answer-8fa1764f-f613-564e-a751-183e5ddece0d" "answer-7d518a4b-9bbd-5ce2-ba1d-3f2676acccd5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-46794791-4cb5-55d4-bbfa-4d50cbc19e9d" :answer/id #uuid "46794791-4cb5-55d4-bbfa-4d50cbc19e9d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6c8a3e36-af4d-58f6-9536-c324e5353465" :answer/id #uuid "6c8a3e36-af4d-58f6-9536-c324e5353465" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d7b129fd-cf43-5f11-9a88-c2df6b3b3d89" :answer/id #uuid "d7b129fd-cf43-5f11-9a88-c2df6b3b3d89" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8fa1764f-f613-564e-a751-183e5ddece0d" :answer/id #uuid "8fa1764f-f613-564e-a751-183e5ddece0d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7d518a4b-9bbd-5ce2-ba1d-3f2676acccd5" :answer/id #uuid "7d518a4b-9bbd-5ce2-ba1d-3f2676acccd5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186069069 :question/problem "Consider the quadratic equation ${x}^{2}+2x-15=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The discriminant of the equation is a perfect square.\n\nII. The equation has only rational solutions.\n\nIII. The equation has only irrational solutions." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have $a=1,$ $b=2,$ and $c=-15.$ So, we calculate the discriminant as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(2)^{2}-4(1)(-15) \\\\  & =4+60 \\\\  & =64\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd $64={8}^{2}$ is a perfect square.\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. \n\nFirst, notice that our quadratic equation has rational coefficients. And, since the discriminant is a nonzero perfect square, the equation must have two rational solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186065847 :answer-field/correct 17592186065849 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-8988f053-e75a-5c75-a866-50d1b2af5964" :question/id #uuid "8988f053-e75a-5c75-a866-50d1b2af5964" :question/math-academy-id "q-218746" :question/problem "The equation $2{x}^{2}+5x-9=0$ has{{field-1}}\ndistinct real solutions." :question/worked-solution "To quickly determine the number of solutions, we can compute the discriminant. We have $a=2,$ $b=5,$ and $c=-9,$ so the discriminant is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & ={5}^{2}-4(2)(-9) \\\\  & =25+72 \\\\  & =97\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the discriminant is positive $(D>0),$ the equation has $2$ distinct real solutions." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9d35e828-7244-5a93-b7ac-e296f8c506d6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9d35e828-7244-5a93-b7ac-e296f8c506d6" :answer-field/id #uuid "9d35e828-7244-5a93-b7ac-e296f8c506d6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-f30d992b-a29e-5752-8a9c-afea2119fa9d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f30d992b-a29e-5752-8a9c-afea2119fa9d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f30d992b-a29e-5752-8a9c-afea2119fa9d" :answer/id #uuid "f30d992b-a29e-5752-8a9c-afea2119fa9d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3d7da907-2808-5b79-bfed-ca1f9e228ecb" :question/id #uuid "3d7da907-2808-5b79-bfed-ca1f9e228ecb" :question/math-academy-id "q-218748" :question/problem "If the quadratic equation $k{x}^{2}+4x+2=0$ has one distinct real solution, then $k=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The equation has one distinct real solution if its discriminant is zero. So, we need to find the values of $k$ so that the discriminant is zero.\n\nFirst, we calculate the discriminant. We have $a=k,$ $b=4,$ and $c=2,$ so the discriminant is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(4)^{2}-4(k)(2) \\\\  & =16-8k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the values of $k$ such that the discriminant is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =0 \\\\ 16-8k & =0 \\\\ 8k & =16 \\\\ k & =\\frac{16}{8} \\\\ k & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the given equation will have one distinct real solution if $k=2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4aa3d16b-4100-574f-84df-1c8d2de4cb31"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4aa3d16b-4100-574f-84df-1c8d2de4cb31" :answer-field/id #uuid "4aa3d16b-4100-574f-84df-1c8d2de4cb31" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-f5241c35-4c53-5fa0-9bb4-acb9e908070a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f5241c35-4c53-5fa0-9bb4-acb9e908070a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f5241c35-4c53-5fa0-9bb4-acb9e908070a" :answer/id #uuid "f5241c35-4c53-5fa0-9bb4-acb9e908070a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-c1dc22aa-5172-525c-b53a-74cc35f15bd2" :activity/id #uuid "c1dc22aa-5172-525c-b53a-74cc35f15bd2" :activity/title "The Discriminant of a Quadratic Equation" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050334 :activity/course 17592186046647 :activity/steps ["step-5e21b816-beb3-56a2-9b03-c6291942c7f6" "step-75aa9ea7-1039-562b-8c94-a84abad863b3" "step-812fd524-4a1b-57bd-bd50-8cf2bc0a2512" "step-82745d32-719e-5bc8-8560-08d4296cae8c" "step-6eeafe58-00df-54fd-bf15-a1b13055fb09"] :activity/first-step "step-5e21b816-beb3-56a2-9b03-c6291942c7f6" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-5e21b816-beb3-56a2-9b03-c6291942c7f6" :step/id #uuid "5e21b816-beb3-56a2-9b03-c6291942c7f6" :step/content 17592186233301 :step/next "step-75aa9ea7-1039-562b-8c94-a84abad863b3" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-75aa9ea7-1039-562b-8c94-a84abad863b3" :step/id #uuid "75aa9ea7-1039-562b-8c94-a84abad863b3" :step/content "question-7966ce86-501b-56f6-9e0c-8b7da65a0478" :step/next "step-812fd524-4a1b-57bd-bd50-8cf2bc0a2512" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-812fd524-4a1b-57bd-bd50-8cf2bc0a2512" :step/id #uuid "812fd524-4a1b-57bd-bd50-8cf2bc0a2512" :step/content 17592186069069 :step/next "step-82745d32-719e-5bc8-8560-08d4296cae8c" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-82745d32-719e-5bc8-8560-08d4296cae8c" :step/id #uuid "82745d32-719e-5bc8-8560-08d4296cae8c" :step/content "question-8988f053-e75a-5c75-a866-50d1b2af5964" :step/next "step-6eeafe58-00df-54fd-bf15-a1b13055fb09" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-6eeafe58-00df-54fd-bf15-a1b13055fb09" :step/id #uuid "6eeafe58-00df-54fd-bf15-a1b13055fb09" :step/content "question-3d7da907-2808-5b79-bfed-ca1f9e228ecb" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186220936 :answer-field/choices 17592186220937] [:db/retract 17592186220936 :answer-field/choices 17592186220938] [:db/retract 17592186220936 :answer-field/choices 17592186220939] [:db/retract 17592186220936 :answer-field/choices 17592186220940] [:db/retract 17592186220936 :answer-field/choices 17592186220941] {:db/id 17592186222042 :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{n}\\} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nuse linearity of Laplace transforms and the first shifting theorem to find the Laplace transform of  $f(t)=5t+3{t}^{2}{e}^{3t}$ for $s>3.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the linearity properties of Laplace transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{f(t)\\} & =L\\{5t+3{t}^{2}{e}^{3t}\\} \\\\  & =L\\{5t\\}+L\\{3{t}^{2}{e}^{3t}\\} \\\\  & =5L\\{t\\}+3L\\{{t}^{2}{e}^{3t}\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that, for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{n}\\} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{t\\} & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}} \\\\ L\\{{t}^{2}\\} & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first shifting theorem states that if $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$ then $L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nL\\{{t}^{2}{e}^{3t}\\}=\\frac{2}{(s-3)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{f(t)\\} & =5L\\{t\\}+3L\\{{t}^{2}{e}^{3t}\\} \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}+3\\cdot \\frac{2}{(s-3)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{6}{(s-3)^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis transform is valid for $s>3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186220936 :answer-field/correct "answer-9fb78983-301f-5651-9fb7-1ab6e18840f8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-83c7c20a-5a9a-5e9c-92d4-1db64ddad7aa" "answer-5c5f41d3-f5c2-5c17-99da-23a331941594" "answer-9a625c27-70a6-5cf6-bcf3-a7be0cbcc47f" "answer-9fb78983-301f-5651-9fb7-1ab6e18840f8" "answer-50798ffc-3732-5591-ba0b-04ece45c7da1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-83c7c20a-5a9a-5e9c-92d4-1db64ddad7aa" :answer/id #uuid "83c7c20a-5a9a-5e9c-92d4-1db64ddad7aa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{(s-3)^{3}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5c5f41d3-f5c2-5c17-99da-23a331941594" :answer/id #uuid "5c5f41d3-f5c2-5c17-99da-23a331941594" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{6}{(s-3)^{4}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9a625c27-70a6-5cf6-bcf3-a7be0cbcc47f" :answer/id #uuid "9a625c27-70a6-5cf6-bcf3-a7be0cbcc47f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{(s-3)^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9fb78983-301f-5651-9fb7-1ab6e18840f8" :answer/id #uuid "9fb78983-301f-5651-9fb7-1ab6e18840f8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{6}{(s-3)^{3}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-50798ffc-3732-5591-ba0b-04ece45c7da1" :answer/id #uuid "50798ffc-3732-5591-ba0b-04ece45c7da1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{3}{(s-3)^{3}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186390920 :question/problem "Consider a function $y(t)$ such that its Laplace transform is $L\\{y(t)\\}=Y(s)$ for $s>{s}_{0}.$ Suppose we wish to find $L\\{{e}^{4t}y(t)\\}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nBy definition of the Laplace transform, we can write \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{4t}y(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-1}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-2}}\\,y(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={{field-3}}\\,\\text{for}\\,{{field-4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Starting from the definition of the Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{4t}y(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{4t}\\,y(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s-4)t}\\,y(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $Y(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,y(t)\\,\\text{d}t,$ where $s>{s}_{0}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s-4)t}\\,y(t)\\,\\text{d}t=Y(s-4),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\ns-4>{s}_{0}\\,⇒\\,s>{s}_{0}+4.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "activity-aee98487-9e85-584a-97ef-6d3596a81ff9" :activity/id #uuid "aee98487-9e85-584a-97ef-6d3596a81ff9" :activity/title "The First Shifting Theorem of Laplace Transforms" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050615 :activity/course 17592186046660 :activity/steps ["step-517ae331-e78e-5e2c-9909-0a4fabd9ed67" "step-5e8902fa-0758-5942-8a51-66f230a0e33d" "step-470da428-0379-5a0d-992c-de8783330311" "step-e30ffbbf-6dc6-5d22-8ffd-fc6caa3397ff" "step-b04bf113-65a4-5948-a558-678fc8e34fe0"] :activity/first-step "step-517ae331-e78e-5e2c-9909-0a4fabd9ed67" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-517ae331-e78e-5e2c-9909-0a4fabd9ed67" :step/id #uuid "517ae331-e78e-5e2c-9909-0a4fabd9ed67" :step/content 17592186222042 :step/next "step-5e8902fa-0758-5942-8a51-66f230a0e33d" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-5e8902fa-0758-5942-8a51-66f230a0e33d" :step/id #uuid "5e8902fa-0758-5942-8a51-66f230a0e33d" :step/content 17592186390920 :step/next "step-470da428-0379-5a0d-992c-de8783330311" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-470da428-0379-5a0d-992c-de8783330311" :step/id #uuid "470da428-0379-5a0d-992c-de8783330311" :step/content 17592186368062 :step/next "step-e30ffbbf-6dc6-5d22-8ffd-fc6caa3397ff" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-e30ffbbf-6dc6-5d22-8ffd-fc6caa3397ff" :step/id #uuid "e30ffbbf-6dc6-5d22-8ffd-fc6caa3397ff" :step/content 17592186221947 :step/next "step-b04bf113-65a4-5948-a558-678fc8e34fe0" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-b04bf113-65a4-5948-a558-678fc8e34fe0" :step/id #uuid "b04bf113-65a4-5948-a558-678fc8e34fe0" :step/content 17592186368081 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105872 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-8d086598-d0f7-52ac-bba9-a6017b092d64"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-8d086598-d0f7-52ac-bba9-a6017b092d64" :question/id #uuid "8d086598-d0f7-52ac-bba9-a6017b092d64" :question/math-academy-id "q-21051" :question/problem "![](images/c8/c85e6ee974a59d956367634fdd1d57b65e9d42a980fe1750886527b173f05d32.png)\n\nFind $BM$ given that the area of $△ACB$ is $49\\,{cm}^{2},$ $AM=5\\,cm,$ and $MC=9\\,cm.$\n\n(Figure not drawn to scale)" :question/worked-solution "The area of the triangle is defined as \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{b\\cdot h}{2},\n$$\n\n where $b$ is the base and $h$ is the corresponding height.\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the height is $h=BM,$ and the corresponding base is \n\n$$\nb=AM+MC=14\\,cm.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the formula for the area, we can solve for the height as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{b\\cdot h}{2} \\\\ 49 & =\\frac{14\\cdot h}{2} \\\\ 98 & =14\\cdot h \\\\ h & =\\frac{98}{14} \\\\ h & =7\\,cm\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $BM=7\\,\\text{cm}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8da7771e-63f1-5e10-be3c-1af25ccedf2e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8da7771e-63f1-5e10-be3c-1af25ccedf2e" :answer-field/id #uuid "8da7771e-63f1-5e10-be3c-1af25ccedf2e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-897c0883-8ea7-5679-b872-375f1438d23b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-68718dec-ecd8-5ae5-a90a-23ccd4586c90" "answer-b854d192-b8ff-551c-8ab1-aef38e4dfe2b" "answer-498f62c4-d10e-5980-af7b-cf193748dc55" "answer-2ed3ec42-7887-59f4-8607-49a2c72ca3d0" "answer-897c0883-8ea7-5679-b872-375f1438d23b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-68718dec-ecd8-5ae5-a90a-23ccd4586c90" :answer/id #uuid "68718dec-ecd8-5ae5-a90a-23ccd4586c90" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10\\,cm" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b854d192-b8ff-551c-8ab1-aef38e4dfe2b" :answer/id #uuid "b854d192-b8ff-551c-8ab1-aef38e4dfe2b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "14\\,cm" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-498f62c4-d10e-5980-af7b-cf193748dc55" :answer/id #uuid "498f62c4-d10e-5980-af7b-cf193748dc55" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "18\\,cm" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2ed3ec42-7887-59f4-8607-49a2c72ca3d0" :answer/id #uuid "2ed3ec42-7887-59f4-8607-49a2c72ca3d0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "49\\,cm" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-897c0883-8ea7-5679-b872-375f1438d23b" :answer/id #uuid "897c0883-8ea7-5679-b872-375f1438d23b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7\\,cm" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105871 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d45bf6d6-3e38-52ed-a276-f99fe5dd268f"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-d45bf6d6-3e38-52ed-a276-f99fe5dd268f" :question/id #uuid "d45bf6d6-3e38-52ed-a276-f99fe5dd268f" :question/math-academy-id "q-87851" :question/problem "![](images/67/678c719915b0d27c1378548267123cfbac3f13f13ee0a05fd6dacd85164911f6.png)\n\nWhat is the area of the right triangle $ABC$ shown above?" :question/worked-solution "Since $m∠A=m∠C={45}^{∘}$ and $AB=BC,$ we have an isosceles right triangle. \n\n![](images/4d/4d340b37161cb3f970ba588fa15a21b3a35ee376c5766e92d6c48358d4ed003e.png)\n\nTherefore, the area of the triangle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}AB\\cdot BC \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {5}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 25 \\\\  & =12.5\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-20a135df-b545-5cd9-8ae5-be9b531bfe60"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-20a135df-b545-5cd9-8ae5-be9b531bfe60" :answer-field/id #uuid "20a135df-b545-5cd9-8ae5-be9b531bfe60" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-417abf8e-a35f-5695-b467-ef4d6b12c3f1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5cfd8bf4-6acb-5b60-99c2-c71861b1536a" "answer-417abf8e-a35f-5695-b467-ef4d6b12c3f1" "answer-b32a6fda-8006-5e0b-bc5e-adba5d208d71" "answer-e8a23dd6-a819-573e-923b-