eb7857abb61f" :answer/id #uuid "e349f683-d5d2-533c-bbf7-eb7857abb61f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e7787ac5-6aeb-5774-9055-b078c9c07d68" :question/id #uuid "e7787ac5-6aeb-5774-9055-b078c9c07d68" :question/math-academy-id "q-101432" :question/problem "What is the $4$th term of the geometric sequence given below?\n\n$$\nf(n+1)=-3.5f(n),\\,f(1)=-2,\\,n\\ge 1\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We use the recursive formula to compute the terms of the sequence up to the $4$th term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =-2 \\\\ f(2) & =-3.5f(1)=-3.5(-2)=7 \\\\ f(3) & =-3.5f(2)=-3.5(7)=-24.5 \\\\ f(4) & =-3.5f(3)=-3.5(-24.5)=85.75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(4)=85.75.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f08de971-253f-5c62-b587-1f127bb77b75"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f08de971-253f-5c62-b587-1f127bb77b75" :answer-field/id #uuid "f08de971-253f-5c62-b587-1f127bb77b75" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-24fc281b-515c-5c55-b365-b208cd62d896" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c65f894c-ad2b-5fd9-88a7-4d2f848bde7a" "answer-ec977cf1-c906-53fd-b5f5-1fb310eb6eda" "answer-a3ad70f6-91a3-5b4a-af2e-ce791c283024" "answer-00bac6ed-4ca1-5d37-a955-d3ac6bd3a6e0" "answer-24fc281b-515c-5c55-b365-b208cd62d896"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c65f894c-ad2b-5fd9-88a7-4d2f848bde7a" :answer/id #uuid "c65f894c-ad2b-5fd9-88a7-4d2f848bde7a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-300.75" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ec977cf1-c906-53fd-b5f5-1fb310eb6eda" :answer/id #uuid "ec977cf1-c906-53fd-b5f5-1fb310eb6eda" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-85.75" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a3ad70f6-91a3-5b4a-af2e-ce791c283024" :answer/id #uuid "a3ad70f6-91a3-5b4a-af2e-ce791c283024" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "125.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-00bac6ed-4ca1-5d37-a955-d3ac6bd3a6e0" :answer/id #uuid "00bac6ed-4ca1-5d37-a955-d3ac6bd3a6e0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "250.75" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-24fc281b-515c-5c55-b365-b208cd62d896" :answer/id #uuid "24fc281b-515c-5c55-b365-b208cd62d896" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "85.75" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e60274a9-ad7a-5782-a789-be03c76f6f3c" :question/id #uuid "e60274a9-ad7a-5782-a789-be03c76f6f3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-249892" :question/problem "Consider the following geometric sequence:\n\n$$\nf(n+1)=\\frac{2}{7}f(n),\\,f(1)=343,\\,n\\ge 1\n$$\n\nThe $4$th term of this geometric sequence is $f(4)=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We use the recursive formula to compute the terms of the sequence up to the $4$th term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =343 \\\\ f(2) & =\\frac{2}{7}f(1)=\\frac{2}{7}(343)=98 \\\\ f(3) & =\\frac{2}{7}f(2)=\\frac{2}{7}(98)=28 \\\\ f(4) & =\\frac{2}{7}f(3)=\\frac{2}{7}(28)=8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(4)=8.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b4c7e41f-ae27-5892-83ef-b3b75e7b59b0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b4c7e41f-ae27-5892-83ef-b3b75e7b59b0" :answer-field/id #uuid "b4c7e41f-ae27-5892-83ef-b3b75e7b59b0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-83d8320e-7ae3-5aee-85eb-74f11662e0df" :answer-field/choices ["answer-83d8320e-7ae3-5aee-85eb-74f11662e0df"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-83d8320e-7ae3-5aee-85eb-74f11662e0df" :answer/id #uuid "83d8320e-7ae3-5aee-85eb-74f11662e0df" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-1faddb6e-7103-543e-9a46-6187e7487263" :question/id #uuid "1faddb6e-7103-543e-9a46-6187e7487263" :question/math-academy-id "q-109811" :question/problem "Which of the three sequences below are geometric sequences?\n\nI. $\\frac{1}{3},\\,-\\frac{4}{3},\\,-\\frac{16}{3},\\,…$\n\nII. $-5,\\,-20,\\,80,\\,…$\n\nIII. $f(n+1)=4f(n)-4,\\,f(1)=2,\\,n\\ge 1$" :question/worked-solution "A sequence is geometric if there is a common ratio between its terms. Let's check each sequence in turn.\n\n- Sequence I is not geometric because not every pair of consecutive terms has the same ratio. To see this, we only need to go up to the third term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{(-\\frac{4}{3})}{(\\frac{1}{3})}=-4 \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}} & =\\frac{(-\\frac{16}{3})}{(-\\frac{4}{3})}=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Sequence II is not geometric because not every pair of consecutive terms has the same ratio. To see this, we only need to go up to the third term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{-20}{-5}=4 \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}} & =\\frac{80}{-20}=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Sequence III is not a geometric sequence. To see this, we can calculate the first $3$ terms of the sequence and then compute the ratios of consecutive terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =2 \\\\ f(2) & =4\\cdot 2-4=4 \\\\ f(3) & =4\\cdot 4-4=12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first two pairs of consecutive terms have different ratios:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{f(2)}{f(1)} & =\\frac{4}{2}=2 \\\\ \\frac{f(3)}{f(2)} & =\\frac{12}{4}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, none of the given is a geometric sequence." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3a716f11-43d9-5db6-97b8-516caa9bdfb0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3a716f11-43d9-5db6-97b8-516caa9bdfb0" :answer-field/id #uuid "3a716f11-43d9-5db6-97b8-516caa9bdfb0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-99df87b4-dd35-52a1-a81f-94fcade66b11" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8b95b35a-939e-5d64-a7a0-741bd14bb687" "answer-8b172897-6256-56d7-a919-3132661e3862" "answer-8dbf5972-ecfe-5bfa-9e97-3f8d36df9174" "answer-223d1a4a-f12e-53dc-b2c2-3f53b62f9cde" "answer-99df87b4-dd35-52a1-a81f-94fcade66b11"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8b95b35a-939e-5d64-a7a0-741bd14bb687" :answer/id #uuid "8b95b35a-939e-5d64-a7a0-741bd14bb687" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8b172897-6256-56d7-a919-3132661e3862" :answer/id #uuid "8b172897-6256-56d7-a919-3132661e3862" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8dbf5972-ecfe-5bfa-9e97-3f8d36df9174" :answer/id #uuid "8dbf5972-ecfe-5bfa-9e97-3f8d36df9174" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-223d1a4a-f12e-53dc-b2c2-3f53b62f9cde" :answer/id #uuid "223d1a4a-f12e-53dc-b2c2-3f53b62f9cde" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-99df87b4-dd35-52a1-a81f-94fcade66b11" :answer/id #uuid "99df87b4-dd35-52a1-a81f-94fcade66b11" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-baaafa36-2999-5769-af9b-da0e59cba7b8" :question/id #uuid "baaafa36-2999-5769-af9b-da0e59cba7b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-101443" :question/problem "Which of the three sequences below are geometric sequences?\n\nI. $\\frac{3}{2},\\,\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\frac{1}{6},\\,…$\n\nII. ${a}_{n+1}={a}_{n}+{a}_{n}^{2},\\,{a}_{1}=2,\\,n\\ge 1$\n\nIII. $f(n+1)=5f(n)+3,\\,f(1)=\\frac{1}{5},\\,n\\ge 1$" :question/worked-solution "A sequence is geometric if there is a common ratio between its terms. Let's check each sequence in turn.\n\n- Sequence I could be a geometric. For each pair of consecutive terms, the common ratio is the same:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{(\\frac{3}{2})} & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{(\\frac{1}{6})}{(\\frac{1}{2})} & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Sequence II is not a geometric sequence. To see this, we can calculate the first $3$ terms of the sequence and then compute the ratios of consecutive terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =2 \\\\ {a}_{2} & =2+{2}^{2}=6 \\\\ {a}_{3} & =6+{6}^{2}=42\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first two pairs of consecutive terms have different ratios:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{6}{2} & =3 \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}}=\\frac{42}{6} & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Sequence III is not a geometric sequence. To see this, we can calculate the first $3$ terms of the sequence and then compute the ratios of consecutive terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ f(2) & =5(\\frac{1}{5})+3=4 \\\\ f(3) & =5(4)+3=23\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first two pairs of consecutive terms have different ratios:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{f(2)}{f(1)} & =\\frac{4}{(\\frac{1}{5})}=20 \\\\ \\frac{f(3)}{f(2)} & =\\frac{23}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, only I is a geometric sequence." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1136a664-65b2-52c5-a34a-dc3466e7ae31"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1136a664-65b2-52c5-a34a-dc3466e7ae31" :answer-field/id #uuid "1136a664-65b2-52c5-a34a-dc3466e7ae31" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-433d90d7-e26f-567c-9af1-c112294a4728" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9fe85af2-0fd8-5185-920a-010f63408182" "answer-433d90d7-e26f-567c-9af1-c112294a4728" "answer-ae09be8b-9fec-50e0-ab6a-459b64c04f25" "answer-fc97ca3f-aeac-59db-8044-ae6fc8558f1b" "answer-3ed8ab5a-274b-5b22-8b12-39cf14734b98"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9fe85af2-0fd8-5185-920a-010f63408182" :answer/id #uuid "9fe85af2-0fd8-5185-920a-010f63408182" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-433d90d7-e26f-567c-9af1-c112294a4728" :answer/id #uuid "433d90d7-e26f-567c-9af1-c112294a4728" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ae09be8b-9fec-50e0-ab6a-459b64c04f25" :answer/id #uuid "ae09be8b-9fec-50e0-ab6a-459b64c04f25" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fc97ca3f-aeac-59db-8044-ae6fc8558f1b" :answer/id #uuid "fc97ca3f-aeac-59db-8044-ae6fc8558f1b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3ed8ab5a-274b-5b22-8b12-39cf14734b98" :answer/id #uuid "3ed8ab5a-274b-5b22-8b12-39cf14734b98" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110720 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d6ec3c46-2316-5f98-be70-c232b5719be1" "question-61992611-6e48-5a2e-9a4c-b280b994c51d"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-d6ec3c46-2316-5f98-be70-c232b5719be1" :question/id #uuid "d6ec3c46-2316-5f98-be70-c232b5719be1" :question/math-academy-id "q-288560" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(r-7)x=-x\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $r?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a=b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=r-7,\\,b=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\nr-7=-1.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $r,$ we get\n\n$$\nr=6.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1e0b4382-89cc-5b45-b568-d6f0542bad8f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1e0b4382-89cc-5b45-b568-d6f0542bad8f" :answer-field/id #uuid "1e0b4382-89cc-5b45-b568-d6f0542bad8f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-987b69a0-afb9-52c1-b363-d34b352bfb3b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0eab2015-3700-56bf-ac92-fd0a94f661e2" "answer-598ab84f-b8ec-5739-8cb0-fcbd36abfb06" "answer-f71c8c9c-cd96-5e6e-b046-0e4cdbb2a210" "answer-96f6e7b6-efcd-5a03-89c8-76e8676edae9" "answer-987b69a0-afb9-52c1-b363-d34b352bfb3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0eab2015-3700-56bf-ac92-fd0a94f661e2" :answer/id #uuid "0eab2015-3700-56bf-ac92-fd0a94f661e2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-598ab84f-b8ec-5739-8cb0-fcbd36abfb06" :answer/id #uuid "598ab84f-b8ec-5739-8cb0-fcbd36abfb06" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f71c8c9c-cd96-5e6e-b046-0e4cdbb2a210" :answer/id #uuid "f71c8c9c-cd96-5e6e-b046-0e4cdbb2a210" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-96f6e7b6-efcd-5a03-89c8-76e8676edae9" :answer/id #uuid "96f6e7b6-efcd-5a03-89c8-76e8676edae9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-987b69a0-afb9-52c1-b363-d34b352bfb3b" :answer/id #uuid "987b69a0-afb9-52c1-b363-d34b352bfb3b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-61992611-6e48-5a2e-9a4c-b280b994c51d" :question/id #uuid "61992611-6e48-5a2e-9a4c-b280b994c51d" :question/math-academy-id "q-288561" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(t+4)x=2x\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $t?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a=b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=t+4,\\,b=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\nt+4=2.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\nt=-2.\n$$" 