"answer-0ae50bd1-5e8a-5b36-a5c0-79150a140e50"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1d19a1e7-c62e-5dd9-8e2c-c2fd148e9c51" :answer/id #uuid "1d19a1e7-c62e-5dd9-8e2c-c2fd148e9c51" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x<3-{log}_{5}⁡(2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a72dc1b4-cf24-53eb-af85-cf883c727da6" :answer/id #uuid "a72dc1b4-cf24-53eb-af85-cf883c727da6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x>3-{log}_{5}⁡(2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0ae50bd1-5e8a-5b36-a5c0-79150a140e50" :answer/id #uuid "0ae50bd1-5e8a-5b36-a5c0-79150a140e50" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x>{log}_{5}⁡(2)-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-d1c3e141-e136-55c6-9d3b-793077b3eeeb" :question/id #uuid "d1c3e141-e136-55c6-9d3b-793077b3eeeb" :question/math-academy-id "q-87888" :question/problem "Solve the inequality ${8}^{2-4x}+11>0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the exponential term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{8}^{2-4x}+11 & >0 \\\\ {8}^{2-4x} & >-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall that a positive number raised to any real power is always positive. Since $8>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n{8}^{2-4x}>0\n$$\n\nfor every real value of $x.$\n\nIn particular, every value of ${8}^{2-4x}$ is greater than $-11.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{8}^{2-4x}>-11\n$$\n\nfor all real values of $x.$\n\nHence, the original inequality is satisfied for all real numbers." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ff518e7d-7337-524b-9fce-f0af17d5e2c9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ff518e7d-7337-524b-9fce-f0af17d5e2c9" :answer-field/id #uuid "ff518e7d-7337-524b-9fce-f0af17d5e2c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-34afea07-154e-5055-aa83-267d189909a3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6225fbff-5d95-5caa-a071-92945444e0c1" "answer-8a652a53-660b-549e-8508-3fead716c4f4" "answer-606b4fd3-98e4-5ae8-9cb9-535434209a8d" "answer-34afea07-154e-5055-aa83-267d189909a3" "answer-0b6a91cd-ec94-5ffa-9fc6-4ea1001fa56c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6225fbff-5d95-5caa-a071-92945444e0c1" :answer/id #uuid "6225fbff-5d95-5caa-a071-92945444e0c1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x<1+{log}_{8}⁡(4)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8a652a53-660b-549e-8508-3fead716c4f4" :answer/id #uuid "8a652a53-660b-549e-8508-3fead716c4f4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x>4-{log}_{8}⁡(1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-606b4fd3-98e4-5ae8-9cb9-535434209a8d" :answer/id #uuid "606b4fd3-98e4-5ae8-9cb9-535434209a8d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x>8-{log}_{4}⁡(1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-34afea07-154e-5055-aa83-267d189909a3" :answer/id #uuid "34afea07-154e-5055-aa83-267d189909a3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "all real numbers" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0b6a91cd-ec94-5ffa-9fc6-4ea1001fa56c" :answer/id #uuid "0b6a91cd-ec94-5ffa-9fc6-4ea1001fa56c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "no solutions" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f9ce2daa-e38d-5bb8-b99f-f6261d8e2ed1" :question/id #uuid "f9ce2daa-e38d-5bb8-b99f-f6261d8e2ed1" :question/math-academy-id "q-365328" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $3\\cdot {2}^{1-3x}\\ge {(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x-4}.$" :question/worked-solution "Taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot {2}^{1-3x} & \\ge {(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x-4} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(3\\cdot {2}^{1-3x}) & \\ge \\operatorname{ln}⁡[{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x-4}] \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+(1-3x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & \\ge (x-4)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+(1-3x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & \\ge (x-4)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+(1-3x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & \\ge (x-4)(0-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+(1-3x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & \\ge (4-x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-3x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & \\ge 4\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-3x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & \\ge 4\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & \\ge 3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡8.$ Since $3<8,$ we know that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡3<\\operatorname{ln}⁡8.$ Therefore, $\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡8<0.$ Because we are dividing both sides of the inequality by a negative number, we must reverse the direction of the inequality sign:\n\n$$\nx\\le \\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6516c76a-3a0c-5cff-b332-8d24558288a2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6516c76a-3a0c-5cff-b332-8d24558288a2" :answer-field/id #uuid "6516c76a-3a0c-5cff-b332-8d24558288a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-27c3cfe2-4355-5ff2-ab48-e1edb2a062fb" :answer-field/choices ["answer-975cd023-3182-5ea3-b55f-a53cbc308e13" "answer-65ce5038-a8fa-57b9-ae79-4389ab61c39d" "answer-27c3cfe2-4355-5ff2-ab48-e1edb2a062fb" "answer-30f97a9c-235e-5b9d-ad94-5f5faf7b30d6" "answer-f340d91e-1c83-5dd3-a48f-6d49a10addf4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-975cd023-3182-5ea3-b55f-a53cbc308e13" :answer/id #uuid "975cd023-3182-5ea3-b55f-a53cbc308e13" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-65ce5038-a8fa-57b9-ae79-4389ab61c39d" :answer/id #uuid "65ce5038-a8fa-57b9-ae79-4389ab61c39d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-27c3cfe2-4355-5ff2-ab48-e1edb2a062fb" :answer/id #uuid "27c3cfe2-4355-5ff2-ab48-e1edb2a062fb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-30f97a9c-235e-5b9d-ad94-5f5faf7b30d6" :answer/id #uuid "30f97a9c-235e-5b9d-ad94-5f5faf7b30d6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f340d91e-1c83-5dd3-a48f-6d49a10addf4" :answer/id #uuid "f340d91e-1c83-5dd3-a48f-6d49a10addf4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-36396874-2543-5347-91a0-563733d2b600" :question/id #uuid "36396874-2543-5347-91a0-563733d2b600" :question/math-academy-id "q-115242" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $-(0.33)^{4x+1}\\le (0.12)^{x-4}.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $(0.12)^{x-4}$ is always positive and $-(0.33)^{4x+1}$ is always negative, we always have\n\n$$\n-(0.33)^{4x+1}<(0.12)^{x-4}.\n$$\n\nThis means that the given inequality is satisfied by all real values of $x.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d5e59730-d10e-5bda-9d7f-0932d9e92317"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d5e59730-d10e-5bda-9d7f-0932d9e92317" :answer-field/id #uuid "d5e59730-d10e-5bda-9d7f-0932d9e92317" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-318198a1-93a0-5e0e-bd4a-c9298fd3a2ac" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ee656d00-69e5-5d75-b16c-4ea445df504d" "answer-9d6fab65-6235-5f96-88f7-f4a6f14f98f3" "answer-75d0224e-d58b-5aa6-bb35-6f8ff9e81486" "answer-318198a1-93a0-5e0e-bd4a-c9298fd3a2ac" "answer-14ea1b4e-9b93-5d12-a61c-da5590d29688"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ee656d00-69e5-5d75-b16c-4ea445df504d" :answer/id #uuid "ee656d00-69e5-5d75-b16c-4ea445df504d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\le 0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9d6fab65-6235-5f96-88f7-f4a6f14f98f3" :answer/id #uuid "9d6fab65-6235-5f96-88f7-f4a6f14f98f3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\le \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡0.12-\\operatorname{ln}⁡0.33}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-75d0224e-d58b-5aa6-bb35-6f8ff9e81486" :answer/id #uuid "75d0224e-d58b-5aa6-bb35-6f8ff9e81486" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡0.12-\\operatorname{ln}⁡0.33" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-318198a1-93a0-5e0e-bd4a-c9298fd3a2ac" :answer/id #uuid "318198a1-93a0-5e0e-bd4a-c9298fd3a2ac" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "all real numbers" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-14ea1b4e-9b93-5d12-a61c-da5590d29688" :answer/id #uuid "14ea1b4e-9b93-5d12-a61c-da5590d29688" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "no solutions" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-1731e842-6496-5676-8984-724a57e1229e" :question/id #uuid "1731e842-6496-5676-8984-724a57e1229e" :question/math-academy-id "q-277889" :question/problem "Solve the inequality ${(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2x}>-(0.52)^{4x-3}.$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$<\\,x\\,<$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Since ${(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2x}$ is always positive and $-(0.52)^{4x-3}$ is always negative, we always have\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2x}>-(0.52)^{4x-3}.\n$$\n\nThis means that the given inequality is satisfied by all real values of $x.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n-\\infty <x<\\infty \n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-54a07fba-ccc2-5984-811e-36401527375b" "answer-field-89ff50ea-6ada-5e88-ad68-fd426c6d4ccb"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-54a07fba-ccc2-5984-811e-36401527375b" :answer-field/id #uuid "54a07fba-ccc2-5984-811e-36401527375b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-d9f12d8b-5eba-5053-9ae2-25dc56f3092e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d9f12d8b-5eba-5053-9ae2-25dc56f3092e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d9f12d8b-5eba-5053-9ae2-25dc56f3092e" :answer/id #uuid "d9f12d8b-5eba-5053-9ae2-25dc56f3092e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-89ff50ea-6ada-5e88-ad68-fd426c6d4ccb" :answer-field/id #uuid "89ff50ea-6ada-5e88-ad68-fd426c6d4ccb" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-067a4aa5-e856-53d1-a787-9e23be107370" :answer-field/choices ["answer-067a4aa5-e856-53d1-a787-9e23be107370"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-067a4aa5-e856-53d1-a787-9e23be107370" :answer/id #uuid "067a4aa5-e856-53d1-a787-9e23be107370" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\infty" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186158507 :tutorial/content "When we have an exponential function in an inequality and the base of the exponential is greater than $1,$ we can take the logarithm of both sides of the inequality.\n\nFor example, to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n2^x < 3,\n$$\n\nwe can take the logarithm (base $2$) of both sides and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡({2}^{x}) & <{log}_{2}⁡(3) \\\\ x & <{log}_{2}⁡(3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reason why we're allowed to take a logarithm of both sides of an inequality is that taking a logarithm preserves the order of numbers. That is to say, if $a < b,$ then $\\log_n (a) < \\log_n (b),$ provided that $n > 1$ and $a,b>0.$\n\nTo see this concretely, consider the following order of numbers:\n\n$$\n0.5 < 1 < 2 < 3\n$$\n\nIf we take the logarithm (base $2$) of all the numbers above, they stay in the same order:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{-1}_{ \\log_2(0.5) } < \\underbrace{0}_{ \\log_2(1) } < \\underbrace{1}_{ \\log_2(2) } < \\underbrace{ \\,\\, 1.585 \\,\\, }_{ \\log_2(3) }\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186158508 :tutorial/content "When we have an exponential function in an inequality and the base of the exponential is *less* than $1,$ we can't take a logarithm of both sides using that same base. To see why not, consider the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2} \\lt 1\n$$\n\nIf we take a logarithm with base $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ of both sides, then we get a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{\\frac{1}{2}}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}) & ≮{log}_{\\frac{1}{2}}⁡(1) \\\\ 1 & ≮0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can get around this problem by always taking a logarithm with a base that's *greater* than $1,$ such as the natural logarithm $\\ln,$ which has base $e \\approx 2.71 \\, .$ Then we can use the laws of logarithms to simplify the result.\n\nFor example, consider the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\left( \\dfrac{2}{3} \\right)^x < 5\n$$\n\nWe can't take $\\log_\\frac{2}{3}$ of both sides, but we can take the natural logarithm of both sides and simplify the result using the laws of logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡[{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{x}] & <\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}) & <\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) & <\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, note that $\\ln 2 - \\ln 3$ is a negative quantity, so when we divide both sides by it, we need to flip the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & >\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186158510 :tutorial/content "When an inequality has exponential functions on both sides, we can often solve it by taking a logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms.\n\nFor example, consider the inequality\n\n$$\n2^{x} < 3^{1-x}.\n$$\n\nTaking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we solve the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{x}) & <\\operatorname{ln}⁡({3}^{1-x}) \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & <(1-x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & <\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 & <\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) & <\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ x & <\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** Other times, it's possible to run into cases where there is no real solution, or where the solution is all real numbers. For example, consider the following equation:\n\n$$\n2^{x} < -3^{1-x}\n$$\n\nSince $2^x$ is always positive and $-3^{1-x}$ is always negative, we always have $2^x > -3^{1-x}.$ This means the above inequality has no real solutions." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157502 :activity/expected-seconds 900.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186158360 :step/math-academy-id 654713 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186158361 :step/math-academy-id 654714 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186158362 :step/math-academy-id 654717 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186158363 :step/math-academy-id 654718 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186158364 :step/math-academy-id 654723 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186158365 :step/math-academy-id 654724 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186158366 :step/math-academy-id 654727 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186158367 :step/math-academy-id 654728 :db/ensure [:step/validate]}]
